WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.130
Andrew Huberman: Welcome to the
Huberman Lab Guest series, where I and

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.890
an expert guest discuss science and
science based tools for everyday life.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.250
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a professor
of neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.909
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.090
Today marks the fourth episode in
our four episode series with Dr.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.050
Paul Conti about mental health.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.110
Today's episode deals with
the topic of self care.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.289
We hear the phrase self care a
lot nowadays, but rarely, if ever,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.489
is self care precisely defined.

00:00:29.969 --> 00:00:33.120
For instance, is self care
about pampering oneself?

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.589
Is it about self acceptance?

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.040
Is self care about just making sure we
get enough sleep and enough exercise

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.430
and have healthy relationships?

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.580
Well, it turns out that yes,
indeed, adequate self care

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.790
is about all of those things.

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:49.060
But true self care, the topic of
today's episode, is about far more

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.519
as it relates to our mental health.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.540
True self care is also about constructing
a life narrative in which we frame

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.940
our past, our present and future in
a way that allows us to see what's

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.179
gone wrong, what's gone right, and
the best path to navigate forward.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.410
So in many ways, true self care is
really about fostering a sense of self

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:12.499
awareness, and doing so within the context
of a framework that is known to work.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:13.440
And today, Dr.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.390
Paul Conti shares with us
exactly how to do that.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.460
He also touches on some of the things
that, if not properly understood and

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.039
processed, can inhibit our ability to take
excellent care of ourselves, including

00:01:25.039 --> 00:01:29.139
how to properly process traumatic
experiences, something that he is expert

00:01:29.139 --> 00:01:30.890
in, among many other topics as well.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.630
He also touches on some of the things
that can potentially serve as barriers

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.660
to excellent self care, including
traumatic experiences, and explains how

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.030
to frame those traumatic experiences
so that we can best move forward.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.200
He also shares with us various
practices that include therapy, but

00:01:46.209 --> 00:01:50.110
also practices that we can carry out
on our own, such as specific forms of

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.539
meditation, journaling, and other ways of
examining the self and fostering better

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.530
self care toward our mental health.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.690
As I mentioned before, this is the
fourth episode in our four episode

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.269
series all about mental health.

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:05.149
I realize that perhaps not everyone has
had the opportunity yet to listen to the

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.840
previous three episodes in this series.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.639
If you haven't, it certainly won't prevent
you from gleaning important information

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.260
and protocols from today's episode.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.240
But I do encourage you at some
point to try and listen to all four

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.730
episodes in this series because at
some level they are interwoven at the

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.179
level of concepts and of practices.

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:24.090
I'd also like to highlight that Dr.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:28.090
Paul Conti has generously provided
some simple diagrams that can help

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:31.120
you navigate today's material and
the material in the other episodes.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.260
They are available as zero cost PDFs by
simply going to the show note captions

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:36.839
where you can view them or download them.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.499
Before we begin, I'd like to emphasize
that this podcast is separate from my

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.119
teaching and research roles at Stanford.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.750
It is, however, part of my desire and
effort to bring zero cost to consumer

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:49.369
information about science and science
related tools to the general public.

