Again, that's wakingup.com/huberman

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.219
.
 And now for my discussion
about mental health with Dr.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.139
Paul Conti.

00:05:34.719 --> 00:05:35.030
Dr.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:36.099
Conti, welcome back.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:36.660
Paul Conti: Thank you.

00:05:36.719 --> 00:05:37.559
Pleasure to be here.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.980
Andrew Huberman: For this series, we've
been focusing on mental health and

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:46.120
really defining what mental health is
and a roadmap to achieve mental health.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:52.810
And in episode one, you laid out for us
a map, essentially, of the things that

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:59.109
any and all of us can look at pretty much
at any time, with essentially any degree

00:05:59.109 --> 00:06:04.360
of resources to try and get a better
understanding of ourselves and how well or

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:09.849
not well we happen to be moving toward or
creating true mental health for ourselves.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:14.469
In addition to that, you spelled out for
us what true mental health really is.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:20.110
And just to recap a little bit of that,
it really boils down to these verb states,

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.000
action states of agency and gratitude.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:23.510
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.700
Andrew Huberman: And then in episode
two, you covered some of the common

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.740
challenges that you've observed in
life and in your clinical practice.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:35.450
And we address some of the ways that
people can overcome those challenges by

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:40.100
going to the map, opening the so called
cupboards as we're referring to them,

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.520
and asking specific sorts of questions.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.900
And then in episode three, we talked about
how looking at the map and exploring the

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:53.069
map in those cupboards in particular can
help people in relational aspects of life.

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:57.300
Romantic relationship, work
relationships, family relationships,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:58.940
and the relationship to self.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.620
Paul Conti: Yes, very important.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:01.600
Andrew Huberman: Very important.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:04.600
Paul Conti: The foundation of all
relationships outside of ourselves.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:07.130
Andrew Huberman: And I'm so glad that
you highlighted the relationship to

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:10.579
self, because today's episode, we
will, of course, return to the map.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.910
And I should mention that if
people have not seen episodes

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.290
one, two, or three, that's okay.

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.690
Today's discussion will be
entirely accessible to them.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.630
But I do recommend that at some point,
they especially listen to episode one and

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.469
hopefully episodes two and three as well.

00:07:24.469 --> 00:07:29.210
But today's discussion is really about
the aspects of ourselves that exist in

00:07:29.219 --> 00:07:35.680
all people and the action steps, the
paths of inquiry that are available

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.779
to all people that can allow anyone
and everyone to improve their mental

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:45.780
health, to move toward these ideals of
agency and gratitude on a regular basis.

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:48.229
And as you pointed out, it is a process.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.150
It's not that we arrive
at agency and gratitude.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:55.840
And just to reiterate, agency
and gratitude are verb states.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:58.670
They involve ways of being in the world.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:00.210
Paul Conti: They're active processes.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:01.960
Like life is an active process.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:04.749
There's not an endpoint
we're trying to reach.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.099
We're trying to live.

00:08:07.429 --> 00:08:10.070
Andrew Huberman: And in thinking
about today's discussion, it occurred

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:14.060
to both of us, really, that today's
discussion is really about self care.

00:08:15.059 --> 00:08:19.979
Self care as a concept, I think, for
many people, evokes notions of like,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.629
okay, you're going to take a vacation
or you're going to kick your feet up

00:08:22.629 --> 00:08:25.660
or get a massage, things of that sort.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.830
And certainly it can involve
those sorts of things.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.735
But just as if we were having a discussion
about physical health and we were

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.870
going to talk about ways to take care
of the physical body to enhance health

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:40.380
span and lifespan, today's discussion
is really about how to take care of

00:08:40.389 --> 00:08:42.189
the internal landscape, the mind.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:42.760
Paul Conti: Right.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.490
Andrew Huberman: Which also
qualifies very strongly as self care.

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:47.030
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.429
Andrew Huberman: So if you would, could
you tell us how you think about self

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:54.879
care, regardless of whether or not you
have a patient who's dealing with severe

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:00.590
mental illness or somebody who's just
hitting the same speed bumps of life over

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.640
and over again or anything in between.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:07.630
What sorts of self care practices
and mindsets do you suggest

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.240
people take on for themselves?

00:09:10.559 --> 00:09:13.330
And for that matter, how do
you think about self care?

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:19.390
Paul Conti: Well, I think we start with
factors that are really just baseline

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:24.680
factors that have to be in place in
order to achieve good things upon them.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:28.620
So the basics of we have to be
eating well enough to feel okay

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.160
and hopefully eating really well.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.230
We have to stay hydrated, we have to
get sleep, we have to move the body.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:38.120
These are basics, but basics a lot
of people are not attending to.

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.990
Similarly, we have to be in
a situation that isn't making

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.069
fear and misery all the time.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:46.340
So an example of an abusive relationship.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:50.320
A person has to navigate out
of that before they can really

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.650
start taking care of themselves
in the way that builds goodness.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:59.589
So we look for the basic factors
that we need to take care of in order

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:03.820
to then look at the factors that
become particular to each of us.

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:07.460
And what we're really looking
for is self understanding.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:10.260
How much can we understand
about ourselves?

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.800
Be knowledgeable about what's going
on inside of us, why it's going on.

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:18.089
Also, and very importantly, being
aware that we don't know everything

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.350
that goes on inside of us, and being
curious about that and looking at how

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.430
we're engaging with the world around us.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:25.910
Do we feel happy?

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:27.119
Do we not feel happy?

00:10:27.139 --> 00:10:30.029
How do we define what happy means?

00:10:30.039 --> 00:10:32.180
How are we engaging with the world?

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:36.600
Because, as you were saying, the
agency and gratitude are verb states.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:38.419
So how are we living life?

00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:39.879
How are we engaging with the world?

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:44.339
Do we feel like life is a sequence
of things I have to do, for

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:47.939
example, or are we doing things we
really, really don't want to do?

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:49.700
Do we have to do those things?

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:50.940
How could life be different?

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:57.640
We start looking at ourselves to assess
how we're engaging with ourselves, the

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:03.200
people, the world around us, in a way that
is either generative or not generative.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:07.920
If we're in that state of agency
and gratitude, then we are going

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.380
to have periods of time where we
feel peaceful, we feel a sense of

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.010
contentment, or we feel delighted.

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:14.879
So is any of that in my life?

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:18.280
If not, why can I start
thinking about that?

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:21.980
Sometimes the answer is quite clear, like,
oh, there's this thing I love, and I'm

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.369
not doing that, right, and I can't do it.

00:11:24.419 --> 00:11:26.860
And then you revisit, like, is
it true that you can't do it?

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.780
A lot of times it is not true.

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:32.160
And if it is true, how does the
person come to terms with that

00:11:32.179 --> 00:11:33.950
process that perhaps grieve?

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:39.390
So say, if it's a loss of a person
that can keep people in terrible

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:41.079
misery over years and years.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.930
So there may be things we have to
understand, we have to process so that we

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:49.370
can get ourselves to that place of knowing
ourselves pretty well and engaging in the

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:54.130
world in ways that we have a pretty good
understanding of and that are adaptive.

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:58.089
And then we look to say, okay,
now how do I make that better?

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:00.139
Because now we're thinking
about preventive medicine.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.569
We want our bodies to be healthy because,
of course, we want to be healthy today.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.855
But we also don't know what will
happen in the future Will there

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.250
be an injury or an illness?

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:13.419
I mean, eventually we all have an injury
or an illness in some way or another.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:17.069
So we're preparing for the
predictable challenges that

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.940
will come our way in the future.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:25.140
And we're well served by doing this
about our mental health, too, right?

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:28.550
There will be challenges that
come our way, there will be losses

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:32.129
and stressors and things that
make us feel bad or feel scared.

00:12:32.559 --> 00:12:34.290
These things will happen to us.

00:12:34.299 --> 00:12:40.080
So the healthier we are, the better today
is, and the better we set ourselves up to.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.710
Either make tomorrow even better today,
or if tomorrow gives me a challenge

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:47.670
I don't have today, I can meet that
challenge and get back to a better place.

00:12:48.389 --> 00:12:50.500
Andrew Huberman: So if I understand
correctly, it sounds like one of the

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:55.900
cornerstones of self care for sake
of mental health involves asking

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.040
really good questions about oneself.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:58.550
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:02.510
Andrew Huberman: I don't think I've
ever heard it defined that way before.

00:13:02.559 --> 00:13:03.059
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:06.700
Andrew Huberman: It's in such stark
contrast to the other forms of self

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:10.079
care, which I certainly subscribe to
as well, like making sure one gets

00:13:10.209 --> 00:13:15.190
enough rest and avoids toxic people
to the extent one can, et cetera,

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:17.819
or toxic environments and so on.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:18.700
Paul Conti: Right.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:21.060
You mentioned ask questions of the self.

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:25.929
But the logical next question to that
is, well, what questions do I ask myself?

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:30.610
Sometimes we know we have
an idea, sometimes we don't.

00:13:30.879 --> 00:13:33.650
And this is where the construction
of a life narrative, like,

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:35.410
let me think about my life.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:39.349
Let me potentially talk about my
life with a trusted other person.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.370
Let me potentially write down
a narrative about my life.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.600
And we can learn so much from doing that.

00:13:46.609 --> 00:13:51.229
So the person who thinks back and
starts to tell a story of themselves,

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:56.480
and let's say, just as an example, that
story is going pretty well, and the

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:59.570
person is feeling pretty good about
themselves, and then say something

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:01.180
happens and it starts to change.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:05.350
Or then this thing happened, and
then I started kind of spending

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:08.870
time with different people, or I
started dating different people,

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:10.900
or I took a different kind of job.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.330
And it can engender the reflection
of, like, oh, things really kind of

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:21.599
changed then, because the emotion
systems within us don't care

00:14:21.639 --> 00:14:23.159
about the clock or the calendar.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:28.390
The emotions, often of negative
experiences, can backmap into our lives.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:32.020
And someone who can tell you, I was
miserable ever since I was a child

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:37.190
can then write out a life narrative
that describes a very happy childhood

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:40.760
until something happened or something
changed at a certain point, which

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:44.680
could be something dramatic, or it
might be increasing pressures of

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:48.910
school, or increasing social pressures,
or how things changed at puberty.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:53.239
And if we have an understanding of
that, we may know the right questions.

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:56.260
Like, for example, let's say
afterwards the person finds

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:57.189
that they're drinking more.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:01.500
An example, but a common example,
instead of taking that for granted.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:02.480
Oh, that's what I do.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:05.209
Or, yeah, I can't cope any better, right?

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.400
The negative things people
will say to themselves.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.350
The narrative can often
point out, I can cope better.

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:11.470
I did cope better.

00:15:11.759 --> 00:15:13.480
I did feel differently about myself.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:18.209
So the life narrative can really
help us establish the roadmap.

00:15:18.209 --> 00:15:21.100
And part of what the life
narrative does is it guides us to

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:23.120
the places to ask the questions.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:27.730
Andrew Huberman: If you would be so kind
as to tell us a little bit more about

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:29.420
how one would do this on their own.

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.320
So does this involve
journaling things out?

00:15:32.469 --> 00:15:37.579
I confess I have a file on my computer
that has a bunch of other files that

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:42.859
starts with age zero to five, and
then I have some notes in there.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:45.790
It's not an autobiography, far from it.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:50.370
It's just highlights of events
that I remember, six to ten

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.150
and so on and places I lived.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:55.660
And I use it just to kind
of orient myself in time.

00:15:56.680 --> 00:16:01.560
I actually don't know what the purpose
and utility of it is, why I initially

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:06.425
started doing this, but it's an important
file to me, and when I return to it, I

00:16:06.425 --> 00:16:08.690
often remember additional key events.

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:10.189
So it's constantly growing.

00:16:10.839 --> 00:16:14.329
I mean, these files are getting
quite large, again, with no specific

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:18.680
purpose of writing this out at
any point, but just to orient.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:19.740
Paul Conti: Right.

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:25.000
You can't not learn about
yourself from doing that.

00:16:25.559 --> 00:16:27.550
It exposes truths of self.

00:16:27.559 --> 00:16:29.390
It makes you ponder about things.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:32.490
It draws your attention to ways in
which you've changed, whether you

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:33.890
think those ways are good or bad.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:36.150
It draws your attention to change.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:39.330
It draws your attention to
the impact of external events.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:41.500
And as you said, it grounds you.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.319
It provides a way of
localizing oneself in time.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:45.610
Like, I am here now.

00:16:46.270 --> 00:16:47.310
Wait, how did I get here?

00:16:47.689 --> 00:16:52.010
And the thoughts and ideas of
how we got here very much help us

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.910
because often we don't do that.

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:57.360
We're sort of rushing headlong
forward, because in many ways,

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:59.380
our society is prompting us.

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:03.439
We live in a very fast moving society and
we want information and gratification,

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:07.020
and often we don't even want it very fast,
but it's coming at us very fast anyway.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:12.740
And to stop and reflect makes a very,
very big difference even to think

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:15.109
at times beyond our generations.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:19.680
To the best of my knowledge, the vast
majority of people on one side of my

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.940
family, everyone was a shepherd for every
generation until, like, two ago, right?

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:28.190
And thinking about that of,
like, that's interesting.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.410
It makes me, in many ways, grateful.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:32.530
So grateful for the opportunity I've had.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.399
But I also think, well, they
lived in close knit communities

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:36.760
then, and what was that like?

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:42.299
And we begin to see ourselves in a broader
way, both in our own history and then

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:47.050
projecting forward, which sometimes is
about children and nurturing children.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:49.419
But it can certainly
be about other things.

00:17:49.419 --> 00:17:50.530
It can be about friendship.

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:51.540
It can be about work.

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:55.750
So we start to see ourselves in ways
that are interesting, that are through

00:17:55.750 --> 00:18:00.560
the lens of truth, and that speak
to our place in the world around us.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:04.340
And I think this engenders
both agency and gratitude.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:06.570
If I'm aware of, like, what have I done?

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.480
What have I accomplished?

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:08.860
When haven't I accomplished things?

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:10.509
How might that be different?

00:18:10.509 --> 00:18:14.479
And a sense of gratitude for being
here and having opportunity and

00:18:14.479 --> 00:18:16.000
even be able to think about this.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:21.050
My guess is when you read through
those files, that at some points,

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:23.970
you have sort of a sense of marvel,
while, like, whoa, that's me.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:24.510
Right?

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:27.790
Whether it's a good memory
or it's a difficult memory.

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.439
No, it's all part of you that
leads you through to today.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:35.219
And you do have a better
sense of self through that.

00:18:35.889 --> 00:18:39.300
Andrew Huberman: One of the feelings
I most often come away from those

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:44.499
excursions into those files with is
one of gratitude, because so much of

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:50.450
what's in those files are recollections
of others that I really appreciate.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:51.860
Some are still alive, some aren't.

00:18:52.309 --> 00:18:58.870
And what that's meant to me and how that
carries me forward, so that's what I do.

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:03.230
I'm sure there are a near infinite
number of ways that people could

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:06.520
do this, but what are a few that
you've seen work really well

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:08.650
that people can do on their own?

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:11.320
Or perhaps with a clinician as well?

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:14.649
In fact, that raises the question,
should people share this sort of

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:18.290
practice and the contents of that
practice with a trusted clinician?

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:24.590
Paul Conti: I think sharing with another
person always should be a trusted other.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:29.920
And we can kind of take stock of that, of
people have an idea of who may be safe.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.220
Often people say, oh, there's no
one I could share something with.

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:36.290
But really, that often comes through a
lens of fear, of exposure, of self, of

00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:40.520
rejection, of vulnerability, which often
is warranted, but sometimes is not.

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:41.709
Sometimes there really are.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:44.370
In fact, often there are safe people.

00:19:44.969 --> 00:19:51.100
So the act of doing something other
than just thinking about something

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:56.780
brings, as you well know, it brings
parts of our brain online that then

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:58.280
are thinking in a different way.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:02.069
So, for example, they may bring
error correction mechanisms online.

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:05.840
So if I'm thinking over and over again
that I've never been good enough to do

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:10.440
anything, that can be just automatic
inside of me, but if I start to write

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:15.489
or to talk or even to formulate words,
to talk to myself or to put words in my

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:21.889
mind as if I were talking now, we come
at it in a different way, and we can sort

00:20:22.059 --> 00:20:26.100
of ferret out the truth within us, which
might be, it's not true that I've never

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:28.100
been able to do things or achieve things.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:32.000
And people often bring that
online by doing something other

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:36.800
than the same thought process
that's gone often over and over.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:41.700
And it's nonproductive, and it brings
down mood, and it raises anxiety, and

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:43.520
it also builds a sense of futility.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:47.210
I mean, I cannot tell you how
often I've heard a person say,

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:48.870
no good will come of this.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:51.669
Or like, okay, try, we'll
try, but I know I can't be up.

00:20:51.719 --> 00:20:55.220
I've been thinking about this for
ten years or 20 years, but what

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:56.749
they've been doing is the same thing.

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.209
They've been ruminating
on it for ten years.

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:04.159
They start talking about it and people
will say, oh my goodness, I've achieved

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:07.550
more in 2 hours than I did in years.

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:11.760
But that's because you're doing
something different in the 2 hours.

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:15.379
So I think that's very important,
especially because we can't say,

00:21:15.679 --> 00:21:18.790
okay, go look in your unconscious
mind and see what you find there.

00:21:19.309 --> 00:21:22.310
So then we need ways of
accessing the unconscious mind.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:26.640
And the communication, either with
self, in writing with others, can

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.770
be very, very helpful in doing that.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:33.629
I am a firm believer
that knowledge is power.

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:38.690
Many times I will feel like I have a
sense of really having helped someone,

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:41.840
and the other person may have that
sense, and we can see the change.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:45.009
And all that I've done is
impart knowledge, right?

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:47.190
We all know different things.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:51.969
So often it's the case that, hey, I happen
to have learned things that are different

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:53.830
from what that other person learns.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:56.480
And then I'm communicating
to them things that I have

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.120
learned, so they know them too.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.330
And then they feel tremendously better.

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:05.380
Because if we put inside of ourselves
the tools of understanding our

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:08.620
unconscious minds, and sometimes
our conscious minds too will work

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:10.539
on them, we'll make use of them.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:16.420
If you talk to a person, for example,
about how trauma can impact us and

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:19.360
how we can shove it underneath the
surface and how it can spin off

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:23.780
shame, then that person may take that
knowledge away and come back with

00:22:23.809 --> 00:22:28.129
real understanding and the fact that
we can do this on our own, right?

00:22:28.129 --> 00:22:29.829
We can do this through good resources.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:35.290
We can do this by taking information into
ourselves that can be very, very helpful.

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:39.390
And it doesn't require, because the first
place to start are with things we can

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:41.930
do that don't require professional help.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:47.330
And sometimes we may come at
problems that do tell us that

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:48.920
we should get professional help.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.280
So if we're having thoughts
of self harm, thoughts of not

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:55.610
wanting to be alive, thoughts of
real despair, thoughts of real.

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:59.710
Of hopelessness, that's telling
us, okay, let's get some help.

00:22:59.719 --> 00:23:03.319
There's a role and a place for
professional help, but people come to

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.440
professional help in other ways, too.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:11.300
Such as, for example, reflecting on
the self real example person thinking.

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:16.219
It really became kind of
different when things started

00:23:16.219 --> 00:23:17.810
changing, like, after college.

00:23:17.810 --> 00:23:21.290
And then I thought like, oh, I've kind
of gotten to this place and I've got a

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:26.113
good job and things really should get
better, but they kind of haven't, right?

00:23:26.113 --> 00:23:27.695
And that was really a branch point.

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:30.189
That person may have never
really thought about that.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:34.740
Or they may have thought about it 10,000
times and then shoved it underneath.

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:38.389
From consciousness to
unconsciousness, because it's a

00:23:38.389 --> 00:23:40.680
scary, vulnerability inducing thing.

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:41.489
It seems scary.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:44.010
Like, how could it be that I achieved
things and didn't get healthier?

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:45.800
Now we're afraid of that, right?

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:51.159
And letting that come to the surface,
being able to say, oh, that's true.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:53.730
I don't have to be afraid
to shine light on that.

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:57.750
Then a lot of times that alone, sometimes
a person will solve their own problems.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:23:58.519
They think about it.

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:00.399
They come in, they have all the answers.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.110
They thank me.

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:02.350
I did nothing but listen.

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:04.280
But the listening part is important.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:07.400
It allowed them to come in and
say what they needed to say.

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:11.080
And other times, then it can be that.

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:12.270
But it's not always that.

00:24:12.309 --> 00:24:15.540
Other times it informs us about
what to work on clinically.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.570
And it might not be
something that's dire, right?

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:19.550
It might just be like, I
want to understand this.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:20.600
I want to be happier.

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:21.270
I want to be healthier.

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:22.790
I want to work towards these good things.

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:26.060
When people talk about that, they're
always, if you really distill

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:27.109
down, what are they talking about?

00:24:27.109 --> 00:24:29.779
A sense of peace, a sense of
contentment coming at the world

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:31.129
through agency and gratitude.

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:35.799
And we can do that through self
inquiry, including through therapy.

00:24:36.260 --> 00:24:39.110
It doesn't have to just be for
situations where, oh, there's

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.080
a significant clinical problem.

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:46.099
Andrew Huberman: Is it the case that
when somebody journals a bit of their

00:24:46.099 --> 00:24:53.089
life narrative or thinks about some great
or sadly traumatic events that perhaps

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:58.060
happened to them at whatever stage of
life, that there is something accomplished

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:04.120
in that action or in that therapy session,
if they're doing it with a clinician, but

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:08.480
that when they go to sleep that night and
perhaps in their waking states as well,

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:14.250
that the unconscious is working some of
that through such, that revelations come

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:17.060
to mind, later insights come to mind.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:21.250
I'm certainly familiar with the
fact that there are certain times

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:25.489
of day and evening where my brain
is in a bit of a liminal state.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:27.380
It feels like somewhere
between sleep and awake.

00:25:27.719 --> 00:25:33.610
And I just have learned that provided
I block against outside sensory input

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:40.149
as much as I can, in particular social
media and the news, that I'll just be

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:43.300
doing the dishes or preparing coffee
or something, and something will come

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:45.639
to mind seemingly out of nowhere.

00:25:46.029 --> 00:25:47.570
It's not always a great insight.

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:51.760
In fact, it's rarely a great insight,
but it always takes me a bit by surprise.

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:54.270
Sometimes a little bit of delight,
sometimes a little bit of shock.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:55.760
Like, wow, where did that come from?

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:57.920
Paul Conti: Because it came from
your unconscious mind, right?

00:25:57.920 --> 00:25:58.790
It was invisible to you.

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:00.869
Then it got thrown up
and you're like, whoa.

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:04.970
And you realize it while in the midst
of doing something relatively mundane.

00:26:05.219 --> 00:26:09.350
Because during the day you're engaging,
your brain is highly engaged, which is

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:14.939
great, but it doesn't leave a lot of
room for the unconscious mind to do its

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:18.270
millions and millions of things a second
that can help you figure things out,

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:20.590
which is the same reason it's uncanny.

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:24.750
Any psychiatrist will tell
you this, that a person will

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:27.090
come in and say, it's strange.

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:33.160
All of a sudden when I can finally
relax, that's when I have a panic attack.

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:34.520
Or they don't know.

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:39.550
Then I can finally relax and I go, then
my heart's beating fast and I'm sweating.

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:41.720
Because that's when
the panic attacks come.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:45.550
If the person is laboring
under something that is causing

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:47.160
them this constant distress.

00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:52.800
When you stop focusing outward and you
sort of settle into an inward state,

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:56.530
then the things that are underneath the
surface are going to come to the surface.

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:59.430
And if there's something really
bothering you that your brain is

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:03.330
very upset about or very afraid of,
what does it throw up to the surface?

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:04.510
A panic attack.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:08.070
But if you're in a good place,
you're taking care of yourself,

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:10.399
you're in a generative stage,
you're in a safe environment.

00:27:10.639 --> 00:27:14.630
Then when you stop putting all
the attention outward, so we

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:16.650
imagine then salience changes.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:19.910
And instead of a lot of the salience
being outward, it starts to be inward.

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:21.379
And you're just sort of meditative.

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:22.410
You're washing the dishes.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:25.889
And there's room then for
your unconscious mind to throw

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:28.580
something important to the surface.

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:33.470
It's the exact opposite of how
people can't remember something

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:34.579
if they're trying to think of it.

00:27:34.579 --> 00:27:37.060
I mean, we all go through this
like, I can't remember that person's

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:41.220
name or that restaurant or whatever
it is, try to keep thinking about

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:42.670
it and see if you figure it out.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:45.129
The answer is not in your conscious mind.

00:27:45.139 --> 00:27:47.500
So if you keep bringing your
conscious mind to bear, you

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:48.760
just generate frustration.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:52.339
But then when you stop thinking about
it, the answer is there inside of you.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:53.820
Oh, I remember now.

00:27:54.920 --> 00:27:58.600
So that's how if we have the
conscious mind engaged in something,

00:27:58.600 --> 00:27:59.710
it's not going to figure out.

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:02.440
Then it doesn't figure the thing out.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.250
And that works for our problems, too.

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:09.280
That's why a person can say, I
thought about that for ten years.

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:12.089
No, you ruminated about it for ten years.

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:15.330
It just ran over and over and
over in the conscious mind.

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:16.940
And how ironic, right?

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:19.639
It prevents understanding.

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:24.049
Andrew Huberman: So it's very clear to
me that asking certain kinds of questions

00:28:24.059 --> 00:28:30.140
about oneself and one's self narrative
life history essentially can be very

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:34.870
beneficial in the moment or moments
of doing that practice as well as the

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:39.720
subconscious, or I guess the appropriate
way to refer to it, is the unconscious.

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:40.450
Right?
Okay.

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:42.910
So for those out there
who, like me, sometimes say

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:44.490
subconscious, it's unconscious.

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:50.730
The unconscious can throw things up to
the surface that can be real insights

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:54.340
can give us not just panic attacks, which
I think most people would like to shy

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:57.530
away from, but as you point out, there's
information in the fact that the panic

00:28:57.530 --> 00:28:59.870
attack is occurring under conditions.

00:28:59.889 --> 00:29:03.380
Paul Conti: And if you stir up the
pot of the unconscious and you put

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:08.580
some new information in, it can do new
things, it can figure new things out.

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:12.580
Which is why the process of
self-reflection, for example, and often

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:18.500
the process of therapy is not always, and
in fact, often is not a pleasant process.

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:23.750
But then we take away from that
hard work, renewed insights.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:25.929
So someone, this happens all the time.

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:26.860
Who knows?

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:32.610
They know that a certain trauma
is inside of them and has been

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:36.390
affecting them, whether it's for
days or weeks or years, they know it.

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:37.610
They don't know what to do about it.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:40.800
They have a conflict about it, so they
keep trying to shove it under the surface.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:45.220
They finally accept, for whatever
reason, to talk about it.

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:50.530
What often happens then is, let's
say during three or four successive

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:54.330
weeks of hourly therapy, that
person is crying and that person

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:56.320
is upset or that person is angry.

00:29:56.320 --> 00:29:58.780
I mean, it doesn't always happen this
way, but it does a fair amount of the

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:02.020
time as they get better and better.

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:04.070
Right, because they're
discharging some of the energy.

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:07.700
Maybe they're crying and they're
sad because they're grieving

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:10.840
something they haven't grieved
before because they've just been

00:30:10.849 --> 00:30:13.579
angry or they've just been ashamed.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:15.199
A classic example is a death.

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:18.390
I mean, how many times do people think,
well, that can't be still affecting me.

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:21.419
It was X number of years ago.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:24.500
But they've never actually
grieved because they carry in

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.510
them, oh, it was my fault, right.

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:29.840
And how many times do we hear that I
should have said something different

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:35.850
before, I should have gone, we then
backmap something that makes us feel bad.

00:30:36.059 --> 00:30:40.840
And then from the guilt and shame
comes the inability to process grief.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:43.410
So if the person then deals with.

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:45.680
Right, I feel so bad about this.

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:48.920
In fact, I feel so ashamed of it
and I feel like it's my fault.

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:50.449
And so.
Okay, well, let's talk about that.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:56.060
Right after my brother's death
by suicide, I felt responsible.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:58.990
I was not involved in
any way in mental health.

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:01.280
I had a business career at the time.

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.720
And I finally went and saw someone.

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:07.430
I wasn't acculturated that getting
therapy was something one did.

00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:11.000
But I realized, hey, I'm not okay, right?

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:13.719
So I didn't know how.

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:18.210
I just knew the manifestation of
it, which was misery and risk.

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:21.869
And I could just tell, I know
what it feels like to not feel

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:23.430
like this and this is not okay.

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:26.350
So then I call the insurance number.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:28.859
Eventually I go in and see a therapist.

00:31:28.889 --> 00:31:33.525
And I'm sure she was a very good
therapist, but she didn't in the

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:36.680
sense need to be, in the sense
that sometimes we don't need to use

00:31:36.890 --> 00:31:39.679
all the things we know we can do,
just something basic with someone.

00:31:39.679 --> 00:31:41.379
And that's all she did with me.

00:31:41.379 --> 00:31:44.300
I mean, she got me talking about it,
and then I talked about how ashamed

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:45.630
I was because it was my fault.

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:46.300
And then really.

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:49.320
And then she challenged me about
that and then in a nice way.

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:54.340
But then it became clear that I was
so utterly shocked by it, right?

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:57.020
Far from it being
foreseeable to me, right.

00:31:57.030 --> 00:32:02.339
That the problem that I was having now
was the shock of it and the sense of

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:06.210
shame and guilt that it raised in me and
then me shoving it under the surface,

00:32:06.219 --> 00:32:10.240
not knowing what to do with it, then
it's making all sorts of misery in me,

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:12.920
and I can't actually grieve, right?

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:17.060
So at some point during those sessions,
now I'm sad and I'm crying, right?

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:19.510
And I know what she was thinking, right?

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:20.170
She's good.

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:20.980
Okay.

00:32:22.049 --> 00:32:23.480
Thank goodness, this
person getting better.

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.629
She probably felt a sense of
relief because she could see,

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:27.240
hey, he's coming out of risk.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:29.139
He's able to feel sadness,
he's able to grieve.

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:30.460
He's been doing this before.

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:34.800
So it's that work.

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:38.810
Wait, if we put into it that makes
a difference, just as with physical

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:41.560
health, I mean, if I want to be
stronger, I want to be more robust,

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:45.550
like I have to go to the gym and work,
or I have to do something that's hard

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:47.180
work, and then I get the benefit of it.

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:50.560
And the same is true whether we're
reflecting on our life narrative and it

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:54.620
brings some difficult emotions to us,
or whether we're talking with someone

00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:56.510
or whether we're doing it in therapy.

00:32:56.510 --> 00:33:01.860
But that's how that process of inquiry
leads us, to take some of the Gordian

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:05.930
knots in us, so to speak, and to cut them
instead of trying to figure out, like, how

00:33:05.930 --> 00:33:09.620
am I going to feel okay about myself, even
though I'm responsible for my brother's

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:11.590
death, because I should have foreseen it.

00:33:11.590 --> 00:33:13.219
I mean, that doesn't work, right?

00:33:13.469 --> 00:33:18.559
You have to say, I see what that thing
is, and that has to go away, right?

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:20.960
And then therapy can lead us to
the point where, oh, it wasn't my

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:22.490
fault, and oh my goodness, I'm sad.

