00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:52.980
I can see how self inquiry
is really powerful.

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:57.570
I've certainly experienced that in my
own life, and it's an ongoing process.

00:34:57.570 --> 00:34:57.720
Right?

00:34:57.720 --> 00:34:59.889
This is not something that
one does and then stops.

00:34:59.889 --> 00:35:02.059
Ideally, you do it forever,
just like physical fitness.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:08.150
And at the same time, I know that
a number of people perhaps are

00:35:08.260 --> 00:35:11.920
wary of self inquiry, especially
because of the pain points it can

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:15.450
bring about and make conscious and
that we have to really sit with.

00:35:16.340 --> 00:35:18.300
And most people would
like to avoid discomfort.

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:23.550
I'm sure there are also people who are
doing quite well in life and therefore

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:25.520
think, oh, pattern of self inquiry.

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:27.840
All it could do, it
sounds like, is more harm.

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:28.930
Like, why would I want to do that?

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:34.409
But I think we both agree that there's
nothing but good and progress and

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:39.959
more agency and gratitude to be had by
going through patterns of self inquiry.

00:35:40.139 --> 00:35:43.859
Paul Conti: I think that really
highlights something very important,

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:47.080
which is that self inquiry
isn't always the right answer.

00:35:47.480 --> 00:35:50.800
Now, I think just because things are
going well, that doesn't mean self

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:52.800
inquiry isn't the right thing to do.

00:35:52.820 --> 00:35:56.560
Self inquiry is always the right
thing to do if we want to understand

00:35:56.560 --> 00:36:01.160
ourselves better, unless we're in a
place where it can bring real risk to us.

00:36:01.460 --> 00:36:05.480
So when I was trying to think about
myself, inquire, why was I so miserable?

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:06.450
What's going on in me?

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:09.699
I reached a point where I
realized I'm not getting myself

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:11.700
anywhere and I'm getting worse.

00:36:11.719 --> 00:36:15.100
And this is not good for me,
because where did this self inquiry

00:36:15.100 --> 00:36:16.509
lead me to more guilt and shame?

00:36:16.879 --> 00:36:20.130
So then at some point, I sort
of pulled the rip cord right on.

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:21.990
It's like, I can't do
this on my own anymore.

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:26.040
And that's very, very important
to anyone who's listening.

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:29.070
If you feel like, look, I don't think
I'm in a safe or a stable place.

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:34.169
Again, thoughts of self harm, thoughts
of hopelessness, then it probably is not.

00:36:34.190 --> 00:36:36.250
Or let's on the side of being cautious.

00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:40.529
It is not a good idea then
to engage in self inquiry.

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:43.000
First go see someone clinically.

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:48.069
And I know that can be hard to do in
this day and age, but if we really

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:52.450
advocate for ourselves, we really push,
we do whatever we can do to try and

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:56.810
get in front of someone who can kind
of help us understand what we may need.

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:59.730
And maybe that person helps us
with the process of self inquiry.

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:01.520
Maybe that person reassures us.

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:06.920
Maybe that person then tells us that we
really do need more care, more help, and

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:10.570
then it leads to us getting that so that
we can come back to the good place of

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:13.050
being well enough for the self inquiry.

00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:17.490
Andrew Huberman: I'm grateful you shared
your path to working with a clinician and

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:21.740
the fact that just focusing on something
on your own wasn't really working.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:26.400
And it sounds like a requirement for a
clinician to help guide you through that.

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:32.020
It relates directly to what I'm most
curious about at this moment, which

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:37.759
is in the map that you established
for us in episode one, and that has

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:39.060
carried through all these episodes.

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:42.049
And by the way, if people are not
familiar with the map, we will

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:46.489
cover it in top contour in a little
bit more depth in a moment here.

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:52.220
But one of the key things, or cupboards,
as we're referring to them, to look

00:37:52.220 --> 00:37:57.140
in in order to exert self care and
improve one's mental health, is this

00:37:57.460 --> 00:38:01.980
notion of self awareness, of really
understanding that there's an I, a me,

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:05.510
and exploring what that's really about
in the moment, but also historically,

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:06.809
through narrative, et cetera.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:13.040
Also in this map is a cupboard
that relates to salience.

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:17.349
What's most obvious, or what do
we default to both internally, in

00:38:17.349 --> 00:38:20.000
terms of what sorts of thoughts we
default to, and externally, what are

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:21.539
we focusing on in the outside world?

00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:26.720
And I think I, and perhaps many other
people out there, are wondering how

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:32.210
to resolve any conflict between a
practice that is aimed at increasing

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:36.700
self awareness and perhaps even drawing
to mind early traumas or challenges or

00:38:36.710 --> 00:38:38.840
recent traumas or challenges and salience.

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:45.879
In other words, if I were to take some
moments or even an hour once a week and

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:49.725
sit there and really think about the
sorts of things that I don't want to

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:54.550
think about that have been gnawing at me
below the surface for a very long time,

00:38:54.550 --> 00:38:59.119
the stuff that I gained some proficiency
at pushing down beneath the surface.

00:38:59.770 --> 00:39:02.290
I think one fear that I have, and
so I have to assume other people

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:06.880
have it as well, is that if I were
to bring that to mind, that it would

00:39:07.570 --> 00:39:09.500
overtake a lot of my waking hours.

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:12.970
It's like I don't want to think
about this thing or those things.

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:15.620
And so now what's salient
is something negative.

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:19.760
And when I'm focused on something
negative, then I'm not able to be

00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:24.390
as generative as I would, like,
move forward toward my life goals.

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:28.670
Now I could even have the realization,
the cognitive understanding that,

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:30.779
okay, but that's necessary, right?

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:35.490
Like, this is like getting a wound
fixed or dealing with a chronic injury.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:37.600
Like, sooner or later, you got
to deal with it, otherwise you're

00:39:37.600 --> 00:39:38.650
not going to be at your best.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:45.259
But that conflict between gaining more
self awareness and also the understanding

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:50.390
that what is most salient to us kind of
defines the quality of our daily life.

00:39:51.929 --> 00:39:55.170
That conflict or friction seems
like an important thing for

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:56.530
us to drill into a little bit.

00:39:56.680 --> 00:39:57.470
Paul Conti: Absolutely.

00:39:57.520 --> 00:39:58.790
And I would say this.

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:03.920
If you think there's something that you
can't bring up into consciousness because

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:07.140
it's going to take over your mind, or
as people often say, I'm going to curl

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:11.110
up in a fetal position, I'm going to
cry and never stop, that is exactly the

00:40:11.119 --> 00:40:18.450
thing you must look at, because salience
presents itself in a whole array of ways.

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:23.629
So if there's something inside of you
that's strong enough that it's throwing

00:40:23.630 --> 00:40:28.269
itself up to the surface, like, hey,
maybe you want to think about me.

00:40:28.269 --> 00:40:33.710
So your unconscious mind, throwing it up
to the surface, that is active in you.

00:40:34.260 --> 00:40:38.540
And often, although a lot of it
happens in the unconscious mind, it

00:40:38.540 --> 00:40:40.259
happens also in the conscious mind.

00:40:40.259 --> 00:40:45.940
And if the person then stops and thinks,
how much might that thing that you are

00:40:45.940 --> 00:40:52.330
not thinking about be impacting you,
how might it be salient in other ways?

00:40:52.330 --> 00:40:54.850
And sometimes a person will realize,
like, yeah, that's on my mind.

