But a key feature of this map is that
while it is very, very complex underneath,

00:55:52.300 --> 00:55:56.420
what emerges from that complexity
gets simpler and simpler, especially

00:55:56.420 --> 00:56:00.610
as we move towards places of better
health and more effectiveness in life.

00:56:01.460 --> 00:56:06.340
So if you would, could you describe
the map in a bit more detail,

00:56:06.420 --> 00:56:09.940
especially what's down there in these
pillars, the complex stuff and the

00:56:09.940 --> 00:56:11.160
stuff that we should be looking at.

00:56:11.360 --> 00:56:15.239
And then we'll touch on some of
those cupboards that we all have

00:56:15.500 --> 00:56:19.250
and the sorts of questions that we
should all be asking in the context

00:56:19.259 --> 00:56:24.140
of some common challenges, but also
some very common and very effective

00:56:24.190 --> 00:56:26.180
paths to doing and feeling better.

00:56:27.380 --> 00:56:31.359
Paul Conti: Yes, the unconscious
mind is the place to start.

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:35.720
That's the deepest level
of the structure of self.

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:42.259
So imagine sitting on top of a biological
supercomputer the size of a house.

00:56:43.210 --> 00:56:45.075
That's what's going on inside of us.

00:56:45.220 --> 00:56:48.380
The unconscious mind is that
biological supercomputer.

00:56:49.360 --> 00:56:53.310
And if we're interested in ourselves,
we become very, very curious

00:56:53.310 --> 00:56:56.010
about what is going on in it.

00:56:56.599 --> 00:57:01.020
And that's where, even though it's not
directly accessible to us, it can be

00:57:01.030 --> 00:57:05.300
accessible through other ways, such as
we talked about reflection or therapy.

00:57:05.300 --> 00:57:10.529
And of course, there are other ways
too, but it is accessible to us, and we

00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:17.340
want to know what is in it, because what
is in it has such a strong effect on

00:57:17.340 --> 00:57:19.630
what's going on in our conscious mind.

00:57:20.180 --> 00:57:28.030
That's the person on top of the biological
supercomputer, the size of a house or the

00:57:28.070 --> 00:57:31.930
image we've been using, is the top of the
iceberg that's coming out of the water.

00:57:31.930 --> 00:57:33.450
So we can look at that either way.

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:38.180
But what is in it has, of course,
a huge effect on the part that

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:40.339
we're aware of, our conscious mind.

00:57:40.350 --> 00:57:45.719
And I think the best analogy here, and
it's actually, I think, quite an analogy

00:57:45.719 --> 00:57:50.169
that parallels very well, is to an
abscess in the field of physical medicine.

00:57:50.520 --> 00:57:54.500
So an abscess is an area
of walled off infection.

00:57:55.170 --> 00:57:59.650
So imagine that there's some infection,
for example, it's often in the abdomen,

00:58:00.150 --> 00:58:04.220
there's some infection, and that
infection could be really dangerous.

00:58:04.500 --> 00:58:08.020
If that infection spread, boy,
it could go to the blood, the

00:58:08.030 --> 00:58:10.679
person could die from that, right?

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:15.880
So the body does a really good
job of walling off that infection.

00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:18.350
And that's a good thing, right?

00:58:18.350 --> 00:58:22.019
Because if the infection weren't
walled off, it poses huge risk.

00:58:22.780 --> 00:58:27.260
But the walled off infection does
not represent a condition of health.

00:58:27.929 --> 00:58:31.549
So someone who has an abscess
in them and doesn't know it.

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:35.610
This happens frequently in medicine,
and we see people coming to emergency

00:58:35.610 --> 00:58:39.089
rooms, and they have a low grade
fever, and they've had a low grade

00:58:39.090 --> 00:58:43.529
fever for a while, and they just don't
feel good, and they have low energy,

00:58:43.529 --> 00:58:46.500
and they're not sleeping well, and
they find themselves sweating a lot.

00:58:46.850 --> 00:58:52.319
There are pervasive experiences going
on that are really detracting from life.

00:58:52.370 --> 00:58:55.670
Like not feeling great all the
time, even though the person

00:58:55.690 --> 00:58:56.960
doesn't feel really sick.

00:58:56.980 --> 00:59:00.980
That's sometimes why it takes a while for
the person to come to medical attention.

00:59:01.270 --> 00:59:05.409
So what's going on is better
than not being walled off.

00:59:05.440 --> 00:59:07.910
But it is not synonymous with health.

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:10.430
So what happens in physical medicine?

00:59:10.610 --> 00:59:13.860
Abscess is identified,
and then someone goes in.

00:59:13.860 --> 00:59:19.190
The surgeon goes in and drains
it, and then the person is better.

00:59:20.099 --> 00:59:21.509
Now, think about that process.

00:59:21.509 --> 00:59:22.650
Like surgery.

00:59:23.209 --> 00:59:25.320
Surgery is not a fun thing.

00:59:25.540 --> 00:59:27.750
Right?
There's anesthesia, there's recovery.

00:59:28.370 --> 00:59:31.170
But surgery is great when
it cures the problem.

00:59:31.210 --> 00:59:31.640
Andrew Huberman: Right.

00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:35.700
Paul Conti: So it's not that, oh, the
physician in the emergency room or

00:59:35.700 --> 00:59:39.089
the family practice doc identifies
that there's an abscess, refer to

00:59:39.089 --> 00:59:41.739
the surgeon, everything is great
and happy, and they're better.

00:59:42.109 --> 00:59:45.379
No, they have to go have a surgery,
and that's not an easy thing.

00:59:45.379 --> 00:59:45.879
Andrew Huberman: Right.

00:59:45.950 --> 00:59:48.310
Paul Conti: But if they go and
do that on the other side, the

00:59:48.310 --> 00:59:50.100
infection actually is gone.

00:59:50.590 --> 00:59:54.999
So they do not have the
symptoms that it was constantly

00:59:55.000 --> 00:59:56.580
spinning off inside of them.

00:59:56.599 --> 01:00:01.230
And they also don't have the risk that
maybe that infection gets out of the

01:00:01.230 --> 01:00:03.820
abscess and their life is then at risk.

01:00:04.570 --> 01:00:10.809
So the parallel is looking into the
unconscious mind to what is inside of

01:00:10.809 --> 01:00:13.899
us that may be acting like that abscess.

01:00:14.309 --> 01:00:17.479
Even though this is an
analogy, it is not theoretical.

01:00:17.949 --> 01:00:20.410
This happens all the time.

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:26.160
And the abscess inside that person
emotionally may be the bullying that

01:00:26.160 --> 01:00:27.970
went on right around the time of puberty.

01:00:28.330 --> 01:00:36.140
It may be that awful boss who is just so
mean and took that good job away from me.

01:00:36.740 --> 01:00:37.840
Andrew Huberman: Right.
Paul Conti: It might be that.

01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:41.730
It might be that assault that
I don't want to think about

01:00:41.730 --> 01:00:43.459
that's really still with me.

01:00:43.470 --> 01:00:46.140
It might be that death I
still feel guilty about.

01:00:46.660 --> 01:00:47.690
I don't know what it is.

01:00:48.130 --> 01:00:52.380
But if there's an abscess in
there, we want to understand

01:00:52.380 --> 01:00:56.020
it and then fix it, cure it.

01:00:56.810 --> 01:00:59.230
And that's what the
therapy process can do.

01:00:59.230 --> 01:01:01.940
And that's why at times,
therapy is unpleasant.

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:03.930
The crying and the anger.

01:01:04.080 --> 01:01:06.110
That's the parallel of
going through the surgery.

01:01:06.110 --> 01:01:06.610
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:10.949
Paul Conti: But on the other side, we've
dissipated the energy inside of it.

01:01:11.150 --> 01:01:13.140
We've taken care of it.

01:01:13.580 --> 01:01:18.320
And that's why it is so important to
go into the unconscious mind if there

01:01:18.320 --> 01:01:23.359
are things that are really troubling
us or if we don't know what's going on,

01:01:23.570 --> 01:01:28.840
to cast a net of inquiry that may lead
us there, because trauma is so common,

01:01:29.029 --> 01:01:34.430
and we shove trauma underneath the
surface because of the guilt and shame

01:01:34.790 --> 01:01:39.830
that it generates, and then it stays
in us like an abscess and spins off.

01:01:39.830 --> 01:01:42.669
Symptoms, they could be symptoms
of diffidence, they could be

01:01:42.670 --> 01:01:45.660
symptoms of overusing a substance.

01:01:45.820 --> 01:01:48.779
They could be symptoms of
avoiding good things in our life.

01:01:49.030 --> 01:01:53.160
But they're pervasive symptoms
that are really harmful to us,

01:01:53.160 --> 01:01:56.439
that we can understand and fix.

01:01:57.860 --> 01:02:02.620
Andrew Huberman: In addition to
quality therapy, what are some other

01:02:02.650 --> 01:02:05.399
ways to access the unconscious?

01:02:05.760 --> 01:02:10.360
Earlier we were talking about journaling
and spelling out one's life narrative

01:02:10.540 --> 01:02:13.960
in written or in spoken form, either
alone or with a trusted other.

01:02:14.400 --> 01:02:18.130
Let's assume that somebody either can't
afford or is just not at the place

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:19.770
where they're willing to do therapy yet.

01:02:20.630 --> 01:02:28.210
But they fully adopt this abscess
model, or this abscess analogy that

01:02:28.230 --> 01:02:33.090
you described, which I think is an
exceptional one, because, a, you

01:02:33.090 --> 01:02:36.700
have the 20 plus years of clinical
experience knowing this exists.

01:02:36.700 --> 01:02:42.049
But also, I think we all, at some level
kind of understand that there's stuff

01:02:42.049 --> 01:02:44.520
happening within us that we can't explain.

01:02:44.860 --> 01:02:50.240
And I, as a neuroscientist, can absolutely
say that most of the neural machinery in

01:02:50.240 --> 01:02:55.279
your head and the parts of it that are
in your body, we don't have access to it.

01:02:55.639 --> 01:02:57.460
We love to think that we do, but we don't.

01:02:57.469 --> 01:02:59.240
It's just clicking away under there.

01:02:59.600 --> 01:03:05.319
So let's say somebody wants to make some
progress, really improve their level

01:03:05.320 --> 01:03:10.079
of mental health, obtain more agency
and gratitude, improve all aspects of

01:03:10.080 --> 01:03:11.800
their life and their generative drive.

01:03:12.320 --> 01:03:15.569
What are some ways that they can
start to tap into the unconscious?

01:03:16.699 --> 01:03:20.930
And my guess is, if it's not in
therapy, it's going to be by looking

01:03:20.930 --> 01:03:24.560
at in some of these other cupboards
as you're describing them, right?

01:03:25.060 --> 01:03:25.560
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:03:25.570 --> 01:03:28.380
Develop and embrace
curiosity about yourself.

01:03:28.740 --> 01:03:36.799
And if you can go for that curiosity,
being dispassionate in the sense

01:03:36.800 --> 01:03:41.400
that you talk about these files
you have with memories and events

01:03:41.400 --> 01:03:46.110
from your past, that's so good,
because you're exploring your life.

01:03:47.230 --> 01:03:51.850
So someone who wants to understand
themselves better, do that for yourself.

01:03:51.889 --> 01:03:56.319
Go look at pictures, talk to people
you knew at different stages of life,

01:03:56.360 --> 01:04:00.550
reflect upon how you behaved at different
stages of life, what you felt inside.

01:04:00.700 --> 01:04:03.480
Anchor yourself to memories and
then extrapolate from there.

01:04:03.710 --> 01:04:07.450
Become curious about yourself,
and if you can, be dispassionate.

01:04:07.450 --> 01:04:10.660
This idea that sometimes
gets called an observing ego.

01:04:10.959 --> 01:04:11.189
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:04:11.189 --> 01:04:13.590
Paul Conti: There are other
words to put to it, but it's

01:04:13.590 --> 01:04:15.040
not ego in a negative sense.

01:04:15.310 --> 01:04:18.550
Here, it means the ability to
stand outside of oneself and go.

01:04:20.110 --> 01:04:24.850
And to really think about oneself
without the negative emotion, we're

01:04:24.850 --> 01:04:29.450
often able to either see the trauma,
for example, or see the change.

01:04:29.459 --> 01:04:34.640
Like, why did I go from feeling
really good about myself, and I

01:04:34.790 --> 01:04:36.180
felt like I could do anything?

01:04:36.180 --> 01:04:40.540
And then just a couple of
years later, man, look at me,

01:04:40.540 --> 01:04:41.980
I'm mopey in the pictures.

01:04:41.980 --> 01:04:44.339
And then, yeah, I was drinking more.

01:04:44.340 --> 01:04:45.640
I stopped taking care of myself.

01:04:46.190 --> 01:04:47.770
That's a pretty big change, right?

01:04:48.860 --> 01:04:50.500
So now we're calling attention.

01:04:50.500 --> 01:04:51.440
What's that change?

01:04:51.440 --> 01:04:53.490
And a lot of times, the person knew it.

01:04:54.230 --> 01:04:55.420
Like, oh, I got rejected.

01:04:55.420 --> 01:04:56.830
I had that terrible breakup.

01:04:56.840 --> 01:05:00.050
And they knew it was a
terrible breakup, but they keep

01:05:00.050 --> 01:05:01.200
shoving it under the surface.

01:05:01.469 --> 01:05:03.519
Maybe they didn't know it
was a terrible breakup.

01:05:03.940 --> 01:05:05.680
Maybe they can't figure out what it is.

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:07.259
That's okay, right?

01:05:07.260 --> 01:05:12.089
Even if they recognize, look, there was
a change, then that will put the lie to

01:05:12.090 --> 01:05:14.330
what in this example is likely going on.

01:05:14.550 --> 01:05:18.569
So that person likely frames
themselves in a way that is very

01:05:18.570 --> 01:05:21.100
negative and always was true.

01:05:21.570 --> 01:05:22.070
Right?

01:05:23.289 --> 01:05:24.500
I can never achieve anything.

01:05:24.500 --> 01:05:25.509
I never feel good.

01:05:25.820 --> 01:05:26.590
No one likes me.

01:05:26.590 --> 01:05:27.339
I can't find a partner.

01:05:27.340 --> 01:05:30.689
Whatever it is we say to ourselves,
it's always been that way.

01:05:31.200 --> 01:05:31.520
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:05:31.520 --> 01:05:34.900
Paul Conti: Because the negative emotion
is so strong, and that part of our brain

01:05:34.900 --> 01:05:38.050
doesn't care about the clock and the
calendar, and then the person goes back

01:05:38.050 --> 01:05:40.059
and thinks, it was not always that way.

01:05:40.699 --> 01:05:42.730
Right?
And it comes to this a lot in therapy.

01:05:42.730 --> 01:05:44.649
It doesn't have to be,
as you said, in therapy.

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:47.529
But, no, I was a go getter.

01:05:47.550 --> 01:05:48.030
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:05:48.030 --> 01:05:50.400
Paul Conti: I'm thinking of the person
who said that I was a go getter, and

01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:54.200
I went out there and did things which
put the lie to her, saying that she was

01:05:54.200 --> 01:05:59.389
lazy, incapable, all those things that
were not true, but she accepted as truth.

01:06:00.029 --> 01:06:02.440
So she needed, from that
mathematical perspective, like, to

01:06:02.440 --> 01:06:04.040
go back and question the givens.

01:06:04.600 --> 01:06:08.720
From our perspective, we're saying,
go look in the unconscious mind.

01:06:08.940 --> 01:06:14.230
Go look in that part of the
pillar of the structure of self.

01:06:14.559 --> 01:06:15.319
Go look there.

01:06:15.320 --> 01:06:18.325
That's the deepest part, the most
complicated part, but it doesn't

01:06:18.470 --> 01:06:19.810
mean we can't understand it.

01:06:20.150 --> 01:06:23.290
And if we start to gain
understanding, then we can think

01:06:23.290 --> 01:06:24.900
more about the conscious mind.

01:06:24.900 --> 01:06:27.179
Like, wait a second,
what am I thinking about?

01:06:27.179 --> 01:06:28.300
What do I think about that?

01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:28.809
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:06:28.809 --> 01:06:30.099
Paul Conti: Have I really
thought about this?

01:06:30.099 --> 01:06:32.480
Or is it just running
over and over in my head?

01:06:32.500 --> 01:06:34.070
What would I like to do about it?

01:06:34.379 --> 01:06:35.709
Maybe I'd like to learn more.

01:06:35.709 --> 01:06:38.430
Maybe I want to go get a book that I think
could help me listen to another podcast

01:06:38.430 --> 01:06:40.410
that could help me talk to a friend.

01:06:40.410 --> 01:06:41.690
Like, maybe I want to do those things.

01:06:41.900 --> 01:06:45.790
So now the flow between the
unconscious mind and the conscious

01:06:45.790 --> 01:06:48.710
mind becomes much more robust.

01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:53.539
And that lets us look further to look at
the next level up, the defense mechanisms

01:06:53.799 --> 01:07:00.490
that grow up out of the unconscious mind,
and we can have some understanding of

01:07:00.490 --> 01:07:02.190
them even though they're unconscious.

01:07:02.630 --> 01:07:07.899
So the idea of wait, when something
shifted in me, how did my way of

01:07:07.900 --> 01:07:10.350
engaging with the world kind of change?

01:07:10.859 --> 01:07:12.139
I was perseverant.

01:07:12.180 --> 01:07:16.990
And I would take some of that
energy in me that wasn't so good.

01:07:16.990 --> 01:07:20.420
And remember, I could put it into
exercising and taking care of myself.

01:07:20.420 --> 01:07:26.059
And then that sort of shifted and
I became sarcastic and cynical,

01:07:26.059 --> 01:07:31.130
and I started avoiding those of my
friends who are really taking care

01:07:31.130 --> 01:07:34.630
of themselves and doing well, because
that made me feel worse about myself.

01:07:35.620 --> 01:07:39.970
That's a dialogue that is
reflective of defense mechanisms.

01:07:39.980 --> 01:07:43.539
Now the person isn't going to
necessarily say, oh, I used a lot of

01:07:43.539 --> 01:07:47.740
sublimation, which is good, and then
I started using reaction formation

01:07:47.740 --> 01:07:49.350
and avoidance just as an example.

01:07:50.810 --> 01:07:55.380
But they're going to understand that
in words, that whatever words they put

01:07:55.730 --> 01:07:59.799
to it, they understand that there was
a change, they put words to that they

01:07:59.800 --> 01:08:01.609
can understand that process of change.

01:08:01.610 --> 01:08:02.359
It's not opaque.

01:08:02.389 --> 01:08:03.850
They're shining light on it.

01:08:04.129 --> 01:08:06.730
And now they can gain a
better understanding of

01:08:06.730 --> 01:08:07.860
it and they can change it.

01:08:07.870 --> 01:08:11.589
Even that realization that I was much
more functional, things were different.

01:08:12.120 --> 01:08:17.719
I mean, that can be a treasure trove
of very relevant, very important

01:08:17.740 --> 01:08:21.719
and very positive information to
bring to the current situation.

01:08:22.319 --> 01:08:26.280
Andrew Huberman: I think we often romance
the idea of the person who can just

01:08:26.540 --> 01:08:31.279
live life forward, who doesn't look
back, who just doesn't really explore

01:08:31.279 --> 01:08:33.240
their past, is just action oriented.

01:08:33.240 --> 01:08:36.120
Because after all, when we wake up
in the morning, all we can control

01:08:36.759 --> 01:08:38.130
is our actions going forward.

01:08:38.599 --> 01:08:42.520
We can't rescript the actions
of past, ours or others.

01:08:43.180 --> 01:08:45.779
Paul Conti: That is such a
good point of what happens when

01:08:45.779 --> 01:08:47.590
we're just looking forward.

01:08:48.140 --> 01:08:52.389
We become like a sprinter who
comes out of the blocks too fast.

01:08:52.399 --> 01:08:58.309
So if you think about the beginning
of a 100 meters dash, they're the

01:08:58.309 --> 01:09:01.809
best sprinters in the world, say,
and there they are in the blocks.

01:09:02.029 --> 01:09:05.340
And if they come out of those blocks
in the right way, they will gain

01:09:05.340 --> 01:09:09.440
momentum, they will keep their form,
and they will run as fast as they can.

01:09:09.740 --> 01:09:13.720
But if they do not pay attention
to what is behind them, the blocks

01:09:13.720 --> 01:09:15.399
that are supporting their body.

01:09:15.660 --> 01:09:19.200
The whole bigger picture here of
the limitations within the body.

01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:21.680
They have to know what
those limitations are.

01:09:21.680 --> 01:09:23.290
They have to understand themselves.

01:09:23.520 --> 01:09:26.899
That's how they avoid coming out
of the block so fast and then

01:09:26.899 --> 01:09:29.059
sprawling headlong onto the track.

01:09:29.240 --> 01:09:30.719
And we see that happen, too.

01:09:30.939 --> 01:09:34.729
So if we're just looking forward and
thought and idea, that's how to live

01:09:34.729 --> 01:09:38.660
life, we will be tripping forward, and
ultimately, we'll be like that sprinter.

01:09:38.660 --> 01:09:41.760
No matter how great a sprinter,
if you come out of the blocks too

01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:43.550
fast, you're going to trip forward.

01:09:43.950 --> 01:09:46.610
Andrew Huberman: Yes, I know I said this
in a different form a few minutes ago,

01:09:46.610 --> 01:09:53.339
but I think a lot of people are afraid
of self inquiry, because they just

01:09:53.340 --> 01:09:58.690
don't want the thing that they discover
which resides in their unconscious, the

01:09:58.690 --> 01:10:04.639
abscess, if you will, or the damaging
thought or thing that happened, which

01:10:04.639 --> 01:10:09.189
they are aware of, but are pushing down
to take over their daily life in a way

01:10:09.190 --> 01:10:13.090
that doesn't allow them to be at least
as functional as they are in the moment.

01:10:13.670 --> 01:10:17.750
Paul Conti: If you go visit the
person who had the abscess cured

01:10:17.760 --> 01:10:21.659
by the surgeon on postop day one,
that person will be less functional.

01:10:22.659 --> 01:10:25.869
They'll be in a hospital bed.

01:10:25.880 --> 01:10:26.324
They won't be able to
get up out of the bed.

01:10:26.324 --> 01:10:27.019
They won't be able to exercise.

01:10:27.950 --> 01:10:29.030
They're going to feel their best.

01:10:29.990 --> 01:10:30.720
That's okay.

01:10:31.680 --> 01:10:36.260
It is okay that we, at times, can become
intermittently, say, less functional.

01:10:36.630 --> 01:10:39.559
In the sense that we're more upset
that I'm spending more time crying.

01:10:40.089 --> 01:10:41.410
That's okay.

01:10:41.639 --> 01:10:45.660
Because that's part of the
energy, the effort, the choice

01:10:45.950 --> 01:10:47.939
that gets us to a better place.

01:10:48.639 --> 01:10:51.820
Andrew Huberman: Ok so it's clear to me
why exploring the unconscious mind can

01:10:51.820 --> 01:10:54.140
be and really is immensely valuable.

01:10:54.180 --> 01:10:56.339
I'm convinced.

01:10:56.599 --> 01:11:01.429
And I can't imagine anyone out there
who would disagree with the idea that

01:11:02.759 --> 01:11:07.580
getting better mentally, being able
to function better in the world as a

01:11:07.580 --> 01:11:13.720
consequence, is not a terrific use of
one's time, even if it, at the surface

01:11:13.720 --> 01:11:19.000
seems to take us off course a bit in
the moment or for even a few days.

01:11:19.280 --> 01:11:23.850
And I think it's also worth highlighting
that it's not the case that if we

01:11:24.010 --> 01:11:27.910
do an exploration of the unconscious
mind, or look in any of these covered,

01:11:27.910 --> 01:11:32.160
for that matter, that our entire day
is going to be overtaken by it or all

01:11:32.160 --> 01:11:33.530
of our sleep is going to disappear.

01:11:33.550 --> 01:11:38.079
I mean, we're not talking about
a process in which everything is

01:11:38.420 --> 01:11:40.519
devoted to exploring these cupboards.

01:11:40.590 --> 01:11:43.040
I mean, there are instances, of
course, where someone hits a crisis

01:11:43.040 --> 01:11:44.260
and they simply can't function.

01:11:44.260 --> 01:11:46.870
But in that case, the thing
they absolutely need to do is

01:11:46.870 --> 01:11:47.790
to look in these cupboards.

01:11:47.790 --> 01:11:48.310
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:11:48.450 --> 01:11:51.530
Andrew Huberman: What are some ways that
we can explore this other cupboard under

01:11:51.530 --> 01:11:57.040
the pillar of structure of self, which
is the cupboard of the conscious mind.

01:11:58.590 --> 01:12:02.460
Paul Conti: So we can also approach
this through the curiosity of self.

01:12:02.950 --> 01:12:07.840
We do a lot of things automatically
that we can stop and think about.

01:12:07.840 --> 01:12:09.570
Like, why do I do that thing?

01:12:10.540 --> 01:12:14.370
And it's amazing what that can provide.

01:12:14.370 --> 01:12:19.659
So, for example, I'm working with
a person who has been going to work

01:12:19.660 --> 01:12:24.900
for a long, long, long time, didn't
need to go to work a long time ago.

01:12:25.860 --> 01:12:30.059
And there's so many other things this
person wants to do with their life.

01:12:30.070 --> 01:12:33.230
They're curious about things, they want
to spend more time with older people

01:12:33.230 --> 01:12:37.809
in their family, but they had to stop
and think, why am I going to work?

01:12:39.190 --> 01:12:41.469
Now he's fortunate enough
that he doesn't have to.

01:12:41.470 --> 01:12:42.240
He also earned.

01:12:42.690 --> 01:12:46.740
He's hardworking, he's diligent, he's
fortunate, but he hadn't thought about it.

01:12:47.240 --> 01:12:50.520
He's been going to work
automatically for a long time.

01:12:50.849 --> 01:12:55.309
And it was the thinking about it that made
him realize, I do that automatically now.

01:12:55.320 --> 01:12:55.650
Why?

01:12:55.650 --> 01:12:59.490
Because it's rooted in unconscious
things but that he's now

01:12:59.490 --> 01:13:01.290
bringing to the conscious mind.

01:13:01.470 --> 01:13:04.030
Because I value hard work
and I value diligence.

01:13:04.730 --> 01:13:09.489
But him stop working doesn't mean that
he's not innately hardworking or diligent.

01:13:09.599 --> 01:13:13.090
He showed that for years and years and
years, and he can show it in other ways.

01:13:13.090 --> 01:13:16.620
Like by he wants to be attentive
to older people who need help.

01:13:16.620 --> 01:13:18.180
There's a lot he can do.

01:13:18.470 --> 01:13:20.150
But he had to go back and look.

01:13:20.309 --> 01:13:23.750
And then, of course, there's a reason
why he didn't realize it, right?

01:13:23.980 --> 01:13:27.530
And even though it's not even a bad
reason, but clearly there was an

01:13:27.549 --> 01:13:33.929
overvalue of hardworking diligent, and
he didn't realize, oh, I've done that.

01:13:33.929 --> 01:13:37.200
I've done enough that I've convinced
myself, I know I'm hardworking, I know

01:13:37.200 --> 01:13:40.530
I'm diligent, so I don't have to sort
of serve that internal master anymore.

01:13:40.780 --> 01:13:41.939
And I can step away.

01:13:42.190 --> 01:13:44.179
And now his whole life has changed.

01:13:44.610 --> 01:13:46.330
But how did the change come about?

01:13:46.330 --> 01:13:50.670
By asking what might one might think
is such a simple question to make no

01:13:50.670 --> 01:13:53.320
sense, like why do I go to work each day?

01:13:53.320 --> 01:13:54.490
Why have I been going?

01:13:54.860 --> 01:13:56.719
He's off on the road to change.

01:13:58.660 --> 01:14:02.990
It's one aspect of how we can
explore the conscious mind.

01:14:02.990 --> 01:14:05.970
It often leads us back to the
unconscious mind, but it's

01:14:05.970 --> 01:14:07.920
awareness of our conscious choices.

01:14:08.510 --> 01:14:10.540
We can also then use tactics.

01:14:10.540 --> 01:14:13.770
So, for example, cognitive behavioral
tactics like thought redirection.

01:14:14.330 --> 01:14:17.490
Like if I'm aware that, hey, there's
a thought that comes into my mind

01:14:17.550 --> 01:14:21.269
a lot, and I start learning ways I
can redirect away from it instead of

01:14:21.280 --> 01:14:22.980
thinking about it 100 times, right?

01:14:23.240 --> 01:14:27.110
And if I learn how to do that, there's
less sort of negative emotion that

01:14:27.110 --> 01:14:30.400
comes from thinking about it and
I can start to feel better, right?

01:14:30.400 --> 01:14:31.599
It's the basic premise of it.

01:14:31.600 --> 01:14:36.059
But these are techniques that can really
help us and they involve understanding

01:14:36.080 --> 01:14:38.210
and guiding the conscious mind.

01:14:39.500 --> 01:14:42.970
Andrew Huberman: I'm smiling because
I'm recalling an experience I had.

01:14:43.380 --> 01:14:48.670
I have a female friend who, very
impressive person, really is overcomeaton

01:14:48.700 --> 01:14:55.230
is a recovered alcoholic for many years
and takes, at least by my read, great

01:14:55.259 --> 01:14:58.349
care of herself and the other people
around her, and has a spectacular sense

01:14:58.350 --> 01:14:59.879
of humor and a bunch of other things.

01:14:59.879 --> 01:15:03.780
But it's probably five, six years ago that
we were in conversation about something,

01:15:03.780 --> 01:15:10.259
I don't recall what, and out of apparently
nowhere she said, I hate being busy.

01:15:11.049 --> 01:15:13.460
And it just stopped me in my
tracks because I'm somebody

01:15:13.460 --> 01:15:15.989
who keeps very, very busy.

01:15:16.240 --> 01:15:19.800
My schedule is extremely full with
things that I really enjoy, some

01:15:19.800 --> 01:15:21.490
things I don't enjoy or enjoy less.

01:15:21.490 --> 01:15:25.620
But fortunately, at this point in my
life, mostly things that I enjoy at the

01:15:25.630 --> 01:15:30.869
time, I was very busy with many things,
including many things I didn't enjoy.

01:15:31.160 --> 01:15:34.000
And her statement just halted me.

01:15:34.250 --> 01:15:38.519
And I realized maybe I
don't have to be busy.

01:15:39.470 --> 01:15:42.180
This whole notion of doing a bunch
of things I don't want to do, sure,

01:15:42.240 --> 01:15:44.930
we have to make our way in the
world and make a living and take

01:15:44.930 --> 01:15:48.470
care of ourselves and others, but
I realized that there was a lot of

01:15:48.559 --> 01:15:50.940
extra stuff that I was doing, right.

01:15:50.940 --> 01:15:54.580
Paul Conti: Because I think what she
meant and what you were reflecting on was,

01:15:55.140 --> 01:15:59.759
I hate being automatically busy, right
? It's not good to be automatically busy.

01:15:59.760 --> 01:16:03.240
And then it makes you think about, wait,
how am I busy in ways that are good for

01:16:03.250 --> 01:16:05.220
me and how am I busy in ways that are not?

01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:07.879
Am I just taking up
time to avoid something?

01:16:07.889 --> 01:16:09.410
You start really thinking about it.

01:16:09.520 --> 01:16:09.930
Andrew Huberman: Yes.

01:16:09.930 --> 01:16:15.100
And the conversation stays with me to
this day, because up until then, I never

01:16:15.100 --> 01:16:19.370
really thought about the possibility
that some or a lot of the things I was

01:16:19.500 --> 01:16:22.139
doing were truly a waste of my time.

01:16:23.219 --> 01:16:28.549
Mostly because I could be putting that
energy into generative things, right?

01:16:28.559 --> 01:16:33.540
Generative tribes, things that would bring
me agency, gratitude, peace, contentment,

01:16:33.550 --> 01:16:35.250
delight, these sorts of things.

01:16:35.640 --> 01:16:38.540
What I'm giving as an example, I
realize, is quite different than

01:16:38.540 --> 01:16:42.036
sitting down in a chair and asking
oneself questions about oneself and

01:16:42.036 --> 01:16:44.890
one's schedule and what one's doing.

01:16:45.150 --> 01:16:46.050
Paul Conti: Same endpoint?

01:16:46.699 --> 01:16:47.199
Andrew Huberman: Same endpoint.

01:16:47.540 --> 01:16:52.920
And I bring it up because I think
it was the fact that it stopped

01:16:52.920 --> 01:16:57.180
me in my tracks, but also the fact
that I can't seem to forget it.

01:16:57.969 --> 01:17:02.020
That means that it must have had
significance, and I would say has

01:17:02.020 --> 01:17:05.959
had significance, because I think
most people are familiar with seeing

01:17:05.959 --> 01:17:08.500
these news articles that come out.

01:17:08.880 --> 01:17:13.250
You know, woman or man, 104, reflects
on what really mattered in life.

01:17:13.250 --> 01:17:15.210
And it's almost always the same things.

01:17:15.210 --> 01:17:16.589
It's like close relationships.

01:17:17.099 --> 01:17:20.160
No one on their deathbed says, I
wish I spent more time at work.

01:17:20.450 --> 01:17:21.529
I might be one exception.

01:17:21.530 --> 01:17:22.750
I actually really enjoy my work.

01:17:22.750 --> 01:17:26.059
So whenever I see that one, I always
think, no, my life without my life's work,

01:17:26.270 --> 01:17:28.390
it would been a diminished life for me.

01:17:29.590 --> 01:17:31.090
I think there are others
out there as well.

01:17:31.340 --> 01:17:34.680
But I think it's very hard for us to
place ourselves into the future of a

01:17:34.680 --> 01:17:38.499
person on our deathbed, looking back,
and then make really good decisions now.

01:17:38.500 --> 01:17:43.300
I think there are ways to do that, but
it seems that it's far more powerful to

01:17:43.300 --> 01:17:45.740
just think about what am I doing now?

01:17:46.060 --> 01:17:52.390
And come to some realizations about
what is really of value now and what

01:17:52.540 --> 01:17:56.680
is of less value or no value now,
and then make adjustments now, as

01:17:56.680 --> 01:17:58.550
opposed to doing the deathbed exercise.

01:17:58.609 --> 01:18:02.090
Paul Conti: You have no other option
if you're going to make change.

01:18:02.090 --> 01:18:07.300
I mean, think about what a complicated
and ultimately meaningless exercise

01:18:07.330 --> 01:18:13.680
it is to try and project ahead into a
future when one is on one's deathbed

01:18:13.929 --> 01:18:15.289
and it's like, what is that like?

01:18:17.150 --> 01:18:19.730
We can't imagine that and
we don't know who's there.

01:18:19.870 --> 01:18:23.870
Whatever that situation may be for any of
us, it's not going to be what we imagine.

01:18:24.070 --> 01:18:29.179
So then we just make something up and we
try and what, extrapolate our lives in a

01:18:29.179 --> 01:18:36.170
way that gets us to this place where we're
on our deathbed and we're not unhappy.

01:18:36.620 --> 01:18:37.110
Okay?

01:18:37.110 --> 01:18:40.750
It just brings us right back to the
future because it's actually simple.

01:18:40.970 --> 01:18:42.800
That is so complicated.

01:18:42.810 --> 01:18:44.140
What are things going to
be like on our deathbed?

01:18:44.140 --> 01:18:45.689
What will happen between now and then?

01:18:46.000 --> 01:18:46.970
All things, I don't know.

01:18:46.970 --> 01:18:48.779
So it's impossibly complicated.

01:18:49.009 --> 01:18:51.919
So then you take it back
to the present, right?

01:18:51.950 --> 01:18:53.080
Like what is it I'm choosing?

01:18:53.219 --> 01:18:57.470
I am the I right now that is moving
through time or is on the luge of

01:18:57.470 --> 01:18:59.140
life or whatever we want to say.

01:18:59.140 --> 01:19:01.679
So what am I choosing right now?

01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:03.270
That's how we make our lives better.

01:19:03.389 --> 01:19:05.939
And we're aware, of course, I
know there's a future I want to

01:19:05.940 --> 01:19:07.160
lead towards a better future.

01:19:07.160 --> 01:19:08.569
I don't have a crystal ball.

01:19:08.570 --> 01:19:10.030
I can't envision what that's going to be.

01:19:10.490 --> 01:19:15.330
But I can do my best now to guide my
life as best I can, and that's going

01:19:15.330 --> 01:19:17.980
to have to lead me to the best future.

01:19:18.220 --> 01:19:21.160
Whatever all the variables
are that I don't know yet.

01:19:21.480 --> 01:19:23.990
Andrew Huberman: The next cupboard
under the pillar of structure

01:19:23.990 --> 01:19:26.920
of self is defense mechanisms.

01:19:27.620 --> 01:19:30.990
I have several questions about defense
mechanisms, but the first question is,

01:19:31.930 --> 01:19:34.730
can we be aware of our defense mechanisms?

01:19:35.380 --> 01:19:37.099
And is there value in that?

01:19:37.350 --> 01:19:41.079
And if so, which defense
mechanisms are accessible to us?

01:19:41.179 --> 01:19:44.160
And I guess the third question
would be, how does one go about

01:19:44.190 --> 01:19:45.909
exploring defense mechanisms?

01:19:47.139 --> 01:19:51.750
Paul Conti: Well, it's sort of fantastical
imagery that there's this iceberg, right?

01:19:51.750 --> 01:19:55.820
Part is underwater, part is above
water, and then from the part that's

01:19:55.860 --> 01:19:58.930
underwater come these sort of branches.

01:19:59.240 --> 01:20:04.789
So the way I imagine it is there are
branches of ice that can be clear

01:20:04.789 --> 01:20:08.460
and have light pass through them
in a way that has high fidelity.

01:20:09.170 --> 01:20:13.410
Or they can be sort of twisted
and unclear and they distort the

01:20:13.410 --> 01:20:14.840
light that passes through them.

01:20:15.120 --> 01:20:19.510
Now they rise up from the unconscious
mind, meaning defenses are unconscious,

01:20:19.510 --> 01:20:25.930
they're automatic, but they're not outside
of our ability to go looking for them.

01:20:25.930 --> 01:20:27.630
They're in the unconscious mind.

01:20:28.320 --> 01:20:31.590
It's not that we can't understand
them, it's that they're elusive and

01:20:31.590 --> 01:20:33.220
there has to be a process of inquiry.

01:20:33.440 --> 01:20:36.509
But we can learn about them just
like we can learn about other

01:20:36.509 --> 01:20:38.680
things in the unconscious mind.

01:20:39.179 --> 01:20:41.599
And here again, knowledge is power.

01:20:41.930 --> 01:20:46.690
So I'm not going to learn anything
new, or I'm unlikely to learn anything

01:20:46.690 --> 01:20:51.390
new about my defense mechanisms if
I don't think about them, right?

01:20:51.610 --> 01:20:55.160
But if I start to think about
them, then I can start to learn

01:20:55.179 --> 01:20:56.890
things and to draw conclusions.

01:20:57.440 --> 01:21:01.320
How am I behaving now
as opposed to before?

01:21:01.340 --> 01:21:02.320
Do I notice that?

01:21:02.510 --> 01:21:03.240
How I'm coping?

01:21:03.240 --> 01:21:06.930
We'll often think coping, but coping
is conscious, but we can access that.

01:21:07.360 --> 01:21:08.140
How am I coping?

01:21:08.140 --> 01:21:11.000
What am I doing and what does it mean?

01:21:11.639 --> 01:21:17.110
For example, someone who after
some difficult experience then

01:21:17.119 --> 01:21:23.009
starts avoiding, can be doing
that without an awareness of it.

01:21:23.040 --> 01:21:28.450
Avoidance is a defense so
avoidance of situations or people

01:21:28.460 --> 01:21:30.319
or potential negative emotion.

01:21:31.400 --> 01:21:36.900
So self reflection can help us understand
which defense mechanisms we're using

01:21:36.920 --> 01:21:39.440
and what may have changed in us.

01:21:39.610 --> 01:21:44.050
So an example, and an example we see
all the time is someone who, say,

01:21:44.050 --> 01:21:49.120
had as a primary defense mechanism,
sublimation before some difficult event.

01:21:49.120 --> 01:21:53.539
And sublimation is taking energy,
taking, say, excess aggression,

01:21:53.740 --> 01:21:55.450
turning it into something positive.

01:21:55.930 --> 01:21:58.820
It's a good way of handling
distress within us.

01:21:58.840 --> 01:21:59.820
It's healthy, right?

01:22:00.220 --> 01:22:04.490
And now, after some change in
their life, they find that, say

01:22:04.660 --> 01:22:09.449
they're drinking more and they're
relying more and more on alcohol.

01:22:09.450 --> 01:22:11.699
And you might say, well,
they're soothing with alcohol.

01:22:12.059 --> 01:22:17.660
Yes, they're soothing with alcohol in
one sense, but what else might that mean?

01:22:18.070 --> 01:22:21.979
And oftentimes what you'll see is maybe
the person is using alcohol because

01:22:22.450 --> 01:22:26.720
they're mad at someone, they're punishing
someone, that someone is probably them.

01:22:26.820 --> 01:22:30.940
They get to have the short term
soothing, but then to feel worse

01:22:30.940 --> 01:22:33.220
about themselves the next day, right?

01:22:33.250 --> 01:22:37.570
And the alcohol is in part a search
for soothing, but it's in part an

01:22:37.630 --> 01:22:40.940
acting out against the self, which
is a different kind of defense

01:22:40.940 --> 01:22:42.909
mechanism that is not healthy.

01:22:43.400 --> 01:22:49.580
So the process of reflection or of
inquiry can help us understand the

01:22:49.580 --> 01:22:52.980
branches that are coming up from the
iceberg, from the unconscious mind.

01:22:53.170 --> 01:22:54.800
How are they in me?

01:22:54.800 --> 01:22:59.530
Are they arranged in a way that's sort of
elegant and they're clear and the light

01:22:59.530 --> 01:23:02.309
is passing through them, or are there
things that have become sort of twisted?

01:23:02.309 --> 01:23:05.119
Okay, what exactly is that?

01:23:05.119 --> 01:23:07.070
How do I go change that?

01:23:07.370 --> 01:23:10.720
I don't want that branch that is
sort of opaque and that the light

01:23:10.720 --> 01:23:12.300
can't get through or is distorted.

01:23:12.850 --> 01:23:13.849
So I can go look at that.

01:23:13.850 --> 01:23:18.190
Because even though defense mechanisms
are unconscious, if I'm working on

01:23:18.190 --> 01:23:22.669
myself, I can take away that, so
to speak, diseased branch, right?

01:23:22.670 --> 01:23:26.880
Or that branch that's not healthy and
put in its place something healthier.

01:23:27.090 --> 01:23:29.610
That's how we can change
our defensive structure.

01:23:29.900 --> 01:23:32.590
Those branches of our defense mechanisms.

01:23:32.590 --> 01:23:37.429
Because even though they're unconscious,
we can reflect on them, bring them to

01:23:37.469 --> 01:23:41.899
consciousness, and then bring ourselves
to bear to make ourselves healthier.

01:23:42.190 --> 01:23:44.190
And it can indeed get healthier.

01:23:44.360 --> 01:23:49.210
And as it gets healthier, it affects
the next level around it, which is

01:23:49.210 --> 01:23:50.920
the person's character structure.

01:23:51.210 --> 01:23:54.229
So, remembering, we're using
fantastical imagery, right?

01:23:54.230 --> 01:23:58.769
Because around the iceberg, below and
above the water, and the branches that

01:23:58.770 --> 01:24:02.350
come out of the part of the iceberg
under the water and how they array

01:24:02.350 --> 01:24:06.360
themselves, we're imagining that
there's a nest that's encompassing

01:24:06.380 --> 01:24:10.230
all of that, the unconscious mind, the
conscious mind, the defense mechanisms.

01:24:10.530 --> 01:24:13.210
And that nest is the character structure.

01:24:13.480 --> 01:24:20.850
It's a way that we contain and define
the self that rides on top of everything.

01:24:21.200 --> 01:24:27.460
It is into that nest that the self
settles and from which the self grows.

01:24:27.889 --> 01:24:32.020
Because the character structure, it's
more than just the conscious mind.

01:24:32.020 --> 01:24:38.250
It's sort of the conscious mind in action,
the defense mechanisms in action, all the

01:24:38.250 --> 01:24:42.330
things that are going on underneath the
surface in the unconscious mind in action.

01:24:42.330 --> 01:24:47.790
And then that's how we be, you
think be as an active word, right?

01:24:47.800 --> 01:24:51.809
That's how we are, or that's how
we actively be in the world, how

01:24:51.809 --> 01:24:53.900
we're engaging with the world.

01:24:54.549 --> 01:24:57.650
Andrew Huberman: So you described the
character structure as the nest that

01:24:57.650 --> 01:25:02.230
is up above the surface of the water,
and that includes things like these

01:25:02.360 --> 01:25:08.179
unconscious defenses and all other
aspects of what comes from below.

01:25:09.420 --> 01:25:14.900
Then you also said that the self,
ourselves reside in that nest.

01:25:16.310 --> 01:25:19.210
I don't recall the exact wording,
but you said something to the extent

01:25:19.210 --> 01:25:22.200
of the self grows within that nest.

01:25:22.219 --> 01:25:22.719
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:25:22.870 --> 01:25:25.549
Andrew Huberman: And as you said that,
I immediately had the image in mind

01:25:25.559 --> 01:25:31.349
of a nest that is either incredibly
nurturing and can really foster the self

01:25:31.350 --> 01:25:35.710
in its best ways and can give rise to
empowerment, humility, agency, gratitude,

01:25:35.720 --> 01:25:39.880
peace, contentment, delight, generative
drive, all these wonderful things.

01:25:40.300 --> 01:25:45.480
I also imagined a nest that isn't as
clean as it could be or that has some

01:25:45.490 --> 01:25:50.600
holes in it, or that isn't stable in
the wind and these sorts of things.

01:25:51.290 --> 01:25:53.910
Is that sort of imagery
that's coming to mind for me?

01:25:53.910 --> 01:25:57.530
Is that a decent way
to conceptualize this?

01:25:57.550 --> 01:26:01.140
Paul Conti: Yes, and I think
it is a very important point.

01:26:01.370 --> 01:26:07.350
The self nests in the character
structure, and from nesting in

01:26:07.350 --> 01:26:09.780
the character structure, it grows.

01:26:10.230 --> 01:26:14.710
We are the self that grows
from within that nest.

01:26:15.400 --> 01:26:18.010
And that tells us a couple of things.

01:26:18.240 --> 01:26:22.279
One, I am something now, right now.

01:26:23.379 --> 01:26:25.039
The things I've done, the
things I've thought, the things

01:26:25.080 --> 01:26:25.740
that have happened to me.

01:26:25.740 --> 01:26:27.300
Like there's a self now.

01:26:27.310 --> 01:26:33.759
So one might think then what grows
out of the nest is what I am now.

01:26:34.080 --> 01:26:36.570
Hence the concept of acceptance of self.

01:26:36.820 --> 01:26:38.000
That's what I am now.

01:26:38.849 --> 01:26:44.030
But I am also responsible
for tending what is growing.

01:26:44.310 --> 01:26:46.030
I'm responsible for weeding it.

01:26:46.740 --> 01:26:50.610
I'm responsible for planting
healthy seeds in it.

01:26:50.830 --> 01:26:55.679
And I think that captures the truth
of the acceptance of ourselves.

01:26:55.710 --> 01:26:58.130
This is what I am now.

01:26:58.139 --> 01:26:59.610
This is who I am now.

01:27:00.029 --> 01:27:03.809
But isn't it beautiful that
I can tend and nurture it?

01:27:03.990 --> 01:27:07.099
And we know, as you'd commented,
what happens if you don't tend it.

01:27:07.100 --> 01:27:08.230
There's a lot of weeds.

01:27:08.429 --> 01:27:09.520
Things aren't going well.

01:27:09.599 --> 01:27:10.809
Things start to get unstable.

01:27:11.259 --> 01:27:12.340
That's not good.

01:27:12.340 --> 01:27:14.630
And we can go that way too, right?

01:27:14.639 --> 01:27:21.019
That's where agency, gratitude, part
of how it all cycles through, right?

01:27:21.020 --> 01:27:24.400
Because our unconscious mind is still
working like it's all still happening.

01:27:24.660 --> 01:27:29.050
And that's how we tend that
garden of the self, so to speak.

01:27:29.050 --> 01:27:34.770
That's how we best tend it, so that
what grows up from it is a self

01:27:34.770 --> 01:27:39.230
that we recognize in the way that
we want to recognize ourselves.

01:27:39.230 --> 01:27:42.170
We see a self that we can feel proud of.

01:27:42.170 --> 01:27:46.019
We see a self that we understand
well enough to guide forward.

01:27:46.270 --> 01:27:51.030
We see a self for which we have
enough respect and humility

01:27:51.210 --> 01:27:54.870
within us to understand that
we don't understand everything.

01:27:55.240 --> 01:27:59.009
And it's from that self that
we engage with the world.

01:27:59.520 --> 01:28:02.639
Andrew Huberman: I've heard many times
before, kind of in the circles of

01:28:02.900 --> 01:28:11.070
psychology and self help and elsewhere,
that we need to all learn to mother and

01:28:11.070 --> 01:28:15.580
father ourselves to some extent, and I'm
not a developmental psychologist, but

01:28:15.580 --> 01:28:20.530
my understanding is that the unconscious
mind, the conscious mind, our defense

01:28:20.530 --> 01:28:25.369
mechanisms, the character structure, all
the stuff that makes up the nest which

01:28:25.400 --> 01:28:30.730
the self resides and hopefully can grow,
are at least at some stage of life,

01:28:30.750 --> 01:28:35.700
perhaps all stages of life, determined
by genetics and by how we were raised.

01:28:35.710 --> 01:28:36.730
Nature and nurture.

01:28:37.150 --> 01:28:41.889
But this phrase, we have to learn
to parent ourselves, is thrown

01:28:41.889 --> 01:28:45.880
around a lot these days, certainly
on social media, but elsewhere too.

01:28:46.139 --> 01:28:51.150
And oftentimes that brings to mind sort
of stereotypes of mothering and fathering.

01:28:51.150 --> 01:28:53.910
And these stereotypes break
down quite a bit these days.

01:28:55.420 --> 01:28:57.519
Things like we have to be
nurturing to ourselves.

01:28:57.730 --> 01:28:59.749
Self respect, self love, self protection.

01:28:59.759 --> 01:29:00.000
Right?

01:29:00.000 --> 01:29:02.059
Healthy self protection
and these kinds of things.

01:29:03.149 --> 01:29:08.880
And all of that sounds fine and good,
but it's always seemed rather vague to

01:29:08.880 --> 01:29:15.580
me if I'm telling myself I'm okay, or
is that mothering and fathering myself?

01:29:15.690 --> 01:29:16.349
I don't know.

01:29:17.579 --> 01:29:21.330
It doesn't seem as concrete as
perhaps I would like and others

01:29:21.330 --> 01:29:25.830
would like because it's not spelling
out to specific actionables.

01:29:26.110 --> 01:29:30.530
What you're describing here makes so
much more sense to me, even though some

01:29:30.530 --> 01:29:34.190
of these concepts are a bit abstract,
because the idea of this nest in which

01:29:34.190 --> 01:29:40.550
the self resides and emerges from
character structure, one can immediately

01:29:40.550 --> 01:29:44.060
see why it's so valuable, and it's
such a key component of mental health

01:29:44.060 --> 01:29:46.570
and self care to tend to that nest.

01:29:46.890 --> 01:29:53.139
And written into that is the fact
that the nest is malleable, that

01:29:53.179 --> 01:29:55.490
we really can make changes, right?

01:29:55.490 --> 01:29:58.830
That we can create a better
internal environment for ourself

01:29:58.910 --> 01:30:00.479
by going through these cupboards.

01:30:00.660 --> 01:30:06.190
Paul Conti: You're pointing out another
crucial factor here, which is if I am

01:30:06.650 --> 01:30:10.570
the garden of self that grows up from
all of it, and I am responsible for

01:30:10.570 --> 01:30:15.499
tending the garden, I'm also responsible
for tending to the whole structure.

01:30:16.190 --> 01:30:21.990
And that's so important if I'm going
to take care of myself in the ways that

01:30:21.990 --> 01:30:27.150
we've talked about, I'm going to tend
not just to the garden that's growing

01:30:27.150 --> 01:30:31.070
out that I can see on the surface,
But I'm going to attend to all of

01:30:31.070 --> 01:30:33.589
me, to the entire structure of self.

01:30:33.889 --> 01:30:38.000
An example here that I think can
illustrate it pretty well is so

01:30:38.020 --> 01:30:40.690
imagine a person who's doing well.

01:30:41.280 --> 01:30:43.900
The part of the iceberg under
the water is solid, right?

01:30:43.900 --> 01:30:45.690
The consciousness on top is solid.

01:30:45.700 --> 01:30:48.339
The defense mechanisms are
clear, the nest is good, the

01:30:48.340 --> 01:30:49.839
garden of self is flourishing.

01:30:50.210 --> 01:30:53.720
And then there's a significant
trauma to that person.

01:30:53.720 --> 01:30:57.400
There's a car accident, someone is
hurt, there's a death of someone

01:30:57.400 --> 01:31:01.270
around them, they have a serious
illness, they lose a job, right?

01:31:01.270 --> 01:31:05.130
It can even be they spent too much
time contemplating and looking at

01:31:05.130 --> 01:31:07.610
news from murders around the world.

01:31:07.610 --> 01:31:10.550
And all the awful things that
we can spend too much time with.

01:31:10.799 --> 01:31:15.285
Something traumatic then goes
into the unconscious mind, right?

01:31:15.520 --> 01:31:19.940
The trauma happens, and what often
happens, not always, but what very

01:31:19.940 --> 01:31:23.889
often happens is the guilt and shame
that are raised cause us to push

01:31:23.910 --> 01:31:25.409
the trauma underneath the surface.

01:31:25.690 --> 01:31:29.680
Now, that's in the unconscious
mind, and it's impacting it.

01:31:29.930 --> 01:31:32.300
And that stability is threatened, right?

01:31:32.300 --> 01:31:36.870
I mean, it's all riding on top of
this giant part of the iceberg that's

01:31:36.900 --> 01:31:38.440
underneath the surface of the water.

01:31:38.710 --> 01:31:43.550
And, okay, we don't have to worry too much
about it if things are going well, but

01:31:43.550 --> 01:31:48.119
if it starts to get fragmented, it starts
to shift, it threatens everything that

01:31:48.120 --> 01:31:54.080
rides on top of it, which is why taking
care of ourselves means taking care of

01:31:54.110 --> 01:31:56.950
all elements of the structure of self.

01:31:57.349 --> 01:32:01.079
Andrew Huberman: That all makes very
clear why tending to the garden is so

01:32:01.120 --> 01:32:06.659
key, and why we as individuals are really
the people most fit to do this, right?

01:32:07.020 --> 01:32:09.500
Of course, when one can, that
work should be done with somebody

01:32:09.500 --> 01:32:12.940
who's a really terrific clinician
to help guide that process.

01:32:12.940 --> 01:32:17.280
And where one can't work with a
clinician, one would hope that they

01:32:17.280 --> 01:32:21.460
would take a structured approach to
this, which is really what we're talking

01:32:21.460 --> 01:32:23.149
about here and in the other episodes.

01:32:23.889 --> 01:32:24.840
Paul Conti: And keeping in mind.

01:32:24.840 --> 01:32:29.880
Keeping in mind that tending to
the self means tending to the

01:32:29.880 --> 01:32:31.840
whole structure of self, right?

01:32:31.850 --> 01:32:34.580
If we keep that in
mind, we won't go wrong.

01:32:34.830 --> 01:32:37.424
We'll pay attention to the surface,
but we'll pay attention to the

01:32:37.469 --> 01:32:38.960
things that are under the surface.

01:32:38.960 --> 01:32:41.669
We pay attention to the
whole structure of self.

01:32:41.980 --> 01:32:44.560
We will shepherd ourselves
forward as best we can.

01:32:45.470 --> 01:32:49.190
Andrew Huberman: I'd love for you to
tell us about the function of self, the

01:32:49.190 --> 01:32:54.769
second pillar that resides alongside
structure of self, and that serves to

01:32:54.769 --> 01:32:59.019
geyser up into how we show up in the
world, hopefully with empowerment,

01:32:59.020 --> 01:33:00.700
humility, agency and gratitude.

01:33:01.050 --> 01:33:02.019
But sometimes, no.

01:33:02.430 --> 01:33:07.880
And as we've established, there
is always, always tremendous value

01:33:07.880 --> 01:33:09.330
to exploring these cupboards.

01:33:09.870 --> 01:33:13.690
So how does one go about
exploring the different cupboards

01:33:13.690 --> 01:33:14.689
under the function of self?

01:33:14.689 --> 01:33:18.379
And we should probably start that
conversation by saying, what are the

01:33:18.510 --> 01:33:20.080
cupboards under the function of self?

01:33:21.630 --> 01:33:24.360
Paul Conti: I'll start off by
saying all the cupboards under the

01:33:24.360 --> 01:33:28.259
function of self will reference the
structure of self, which makes sense.

01:33:28.270 --> 01:33:32.400
There's a structure, and the
function arises from the structure.

01:33:32.889 --> 01:33:35.260
It's good for us to have that in
mind as we're thinking about the

01:33:35.270 --> 01:33:37.550
elements of the function of self.

01:33:37.830 --> 01:33:42.870
So the deepest element, let's
say the bottom of the pillar,

01:33:43.420 --> 01:33:45.619
is self awareness, right?

01:33:45.630 --> 01:33:49.580
The sense of an I on top of that.

01:33:50.070 --> 01:33:54.929
Next up the pillar are
defense mechanisms in action.

01:33:56.089 --> 01:33:58.470
Up from that is salience.

01:33:58.600 --> 01:34:01.370
What we're paying attention
to inside and out.

01:34:01.910 --> 01:34:07.679
The next level above that is behavior,
and on top of that is our strivings.

01:34:08.820 --> 01:34:13.790
So if we go back to the bottom
layer, the deepest, most complicated

01:34:14.200 --> 01:34:18.290
layer, it's the sense of self
awareness, the sense of an I.

01:34:18.800 --> 01:34:23.330
And there are a lot of ways that
we can foster self awareness.

01:34:23.520 --> 01:34:28.010
So, like the unconscious mind in the
structure, we can't just go there and

01:34:28.020 --> 01:34:34.760
fully understand what the I is, but we can
do things that can really, really help us.

01:34:34.770 --> 01:34:40.139
So for me, thinking about what am
I, and how am I navigating the world

01:34:40.139 --> 01:34:43.369
and having in mind the structure
of self, like, right, there's an

01:34:43.380 --> 01:34:45.460
unconscious mind working its way in me.

01:34:45.460 --> 01:34:46.539
There's my conscious mind.

01:34:46.760 --> 01:34:52.170
Even being aware of the first pillar
can be part of fostering the self

01:34:52.170 --> 01:34:54.880
awareness of the second pillar.

01:34:55.190 --> 01:34:57.800
Another way that can
happen is self reflection.

01:34:57.809 --> 01:35:01.700
For some people, it can happen in
meditation, contemplation of the self.

01:35:02.170 --> 01:35:05.989
There are many ways that we can
help ourselves understand that

01:35:06.060 --> 01:35:07.760
living is an active process.

01:35:07.760 --> 01:35:13.810
That idea of the luge of time and
we're moving down it, it's an active

01:35:13.840 --> 01:35:18.659
process, and that is the I that
I'm guiding through that process.

01:35:18.960 --> 01:35:22.949
We can foster self awareness in
a number of ways, but what we're

01:35:22.949 --> 01:35:28.460
trying to do here, the same as with
the bottom of the structure of self,

01:35:28.460 --> 01:35:30.340
pillar the most complicated parts.

01:35:30.580 --> 01:35:32.170
There's a lot that's unconscious.

01:35:32.170 --> 01:35:33.830
There's a lot that's unknown to us.

01:35:34.020 --> 01:35:39.279
So what we're trying to do is know some
of it and know more of it over time.

01:35:39.580 --> 01:35:43.250
Bring some of those automatic
or unconscious things to

01:35:43.270 --> 01:35:46.259
conscious awareness so that we
can have a better understanding.

01:35:46.280 --> 01:35:48.910
Because if we have an
understanding, we can utilize

01:35:48.910 --> 01:35:50.780
that to make everything better.

01:35:51.179 --> 01:35:55.079
Andrew Huberman: I can see right off how
this first cupboard of self awareness and

01:35:55.099 --> 01:35:57.989
an exploration of the eye is so critical.

01:35:58.250 --> 01:36:02.009
And realizing that we have a physical
body, that we have agency in the

01:36:02.010 --> 01:36:04.050
world to do least certain things.

01:36:05.090 --> 01:36:09.939
And in an earlier episode, you mentioned
a practice, actually, of looking in the

01:36:09.940 --> 01:36:14.920
mirror and focusing on this reality,
that we have a physical body, we

01:36:14.920 --> 01:36:16.650
reside in it, and then we have agency.

01:36:16.650 --> 01:36:20.490
We can do things in the world as
a way to reinforce self awareness.

01:36:21.080 --> 01:36:24.920
Such an interesting practice, and one
that I started on immediately after.

01:36:25.550 --> 01:36:26.540
Paul Conti: Really?
Andrew Huberman: Well, that evening.

01:36:26.550 --> 01:36:26.910
Yeah.

01:36:26.910 --> 01:36:30.550
And the next morning, after hearing it
from you, some interesting things came to

01:36:30.550 --> 01:36:32.230
mind, and I encourage people to try it.

01:36:32.230 --> 01:36:33.599
It's done.

01:36:33.620 --> 01:36:34.400
Eyes opened.

01:36:34.539 --> 01:36:35.930
Just for a few minutes or so.

01:36:35.930 --> 01:36:36.720
Two, three minutes.

01:36:36.720 --> 01:36:42.790
In my case, some interesting understanding
came about, especially when coupled

01:36:42.790 --> 01:36:46.360
with thinking about some of my life
narrative and things that have happened.

01:36:46.360 --> 01:36:50.530
So I highly recommend people explore
this practice that you described.

01:36:51.630 --> 01:36:55.420
I'm also interested in the sorts of
narratives that we have about ourselves.

01:36:56.400 --> 01:37:00.290
I think everyone has narratives about
what they're good at, what they're

01:37:00.290 --> 01:37:03.340
less good at, what's happened to
them, why it's happened to them.

01:37:04.340 --> 01:37:07.929
Could you tell us what you think
about exploring our narratives?

01:37:07.929 --> 01:37:11.220
Not just exploring the fact that we
have a physical body, but exploring

01:37:11.220 --> 01:37:13.870
our stories about ourselves.

01:37:15.100 --> 01:37:20.060
Paul Conti: Well, self awareness
is just the awareness of an I.

01:37:20.270 --> 01:37:23.240
So we can use our conscious
mind to help that.

01:37:23.500 --> 01:37:29.879
So this aspect of function of self
isn't about what the narrative means.

01:37:30.570 --> 01:37:31.940
That comes later.

01:37:32.250 --> 01:37:35.305
This is about the awareness of an I.

01:37:35.540 --> 01:37:39.349
So when you were talking about the
narrative, you said something along

01:37:39.349 --> 01:37:42.669
the lines of, like, there's stories,
and you're not thinking of, like, oh,

01:37:42.929 --> 01:37:45.230
it's the same me in these stories.

01:37:45.509 --> 01:37:49.240
If you approached the narrative
in a different way, the awareness,

01:37:49.420 --> 01:37:51.179
like, there's an I, right?

01:37:51.190 --> 01:37:51.780
There's a me.

01:37:51.780 --> 01:37:56.280
Like, I'm the point of all these
stories.That's why they're here.

01:37:56.280 --> 01:38:01.000
They're all in me in some way or another
because I remember them, and they're

01:38:01.000 --> 01:38:02.939
important enough that I wrote them down.

01:38:03.230 --> 01:38:08.200
If you look at it that way, where you're
just apprehending an I, like, there's

01:38:08.200 --> 01:38:10.920
a me, to whom all of this applies.

01:38:11.310 --> 01:38:14.320
That's how we can use the
conscious mind and the narrative

01:38:14.590 --> 01:38:16.380
in order to foster self awareness.

01:38:16.380 --> 01:38:18.110
It's not yet about meaning.

01:38:18.120 --> 01:38:19.889
It's about the awareness of an I.

01:38:20.679 --> 01:38:23.260
Andrew Huberman: So it's actually much
simpler than I'm making it out to be.

01:38:23.350 --> 01:38:24.120
At some level?

01:38:24.199 --> 01:38:25.339
Paul Conti: At that level, yes.

01:38:25.580 --> 01:38:26.080
Andrew Huberman: Got it.

01:38:26.260 --> 01:38:26.760
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:38:26.910 --> 01:38:27.120
Ok.

01:38:27.120 --> 01:38:28.390
Andrew Huberman: Well, then
at some point, we will return

01:38:28.390 --> 01:38:31.309
to this theme of narratives.

01:38:31.390 --> 01:38:34.219
Narratives that serve us, perhaps
narratives that don't serve us.

01:38:34.959 --> 01:38:40.380
Meanwhile, take us into that second
bin under the function of self,

01:38:40.680 --> 01:38:42.880
the defense mechanisms in action.

01:38:43.080 --> 01:38:46.400
I find these infinitely fascinating,
and I think many other people do

01:38:46.400 --> 01:38:53.620
too, because sublimation, denial,
these kinds of things, they really

01:38:53.679 --> 01:38:57.160
provide so much of what does and
doesn't happen to each of us.

01:38:59.350 --> 01:39:04.330
If you could tell us how we can think
about our defense mechanisms in action

01:39:04.350 --> 01:39:06.780
in a way that can improve our health.

01:39:06.910 --> 01:39:08.480
Paul Conti: Yeah, of course.

01:39:08.480 --> 01:39:11.799
Defense mechanisms are
under the structure of mind.

01:39:11.830 --> 01:39:15.249
Defense mechanisms in action
are under function of mind.

01:39:15.710 --> 01:39:20.210
They're unconscious processes
that we can gain sometimes a very

01:39:20.210 --> 01:39:24.200
good understanding of by directing
our conscious mind towards them.

01:39:24.590 --> 01:39:27.870
And this is a place where we
can use narratives, we can

01:39:27.870 --> 01:39:29.180
use an understanding of self.

01:39:29.180 --> 01:39:33.609
So as an example, someone who's
thinking about themselves and what

01:39:33.610 --> 01:39:36.719
they want to do for a living, if they
want their job or where they want to

01:39:36.750 --> 01:39:42.820
live, and who's thinking about self
can realize he's going to feel good

01:39:43.520 --> 01:39:45.250
when I'm doing something for someone.

01:39:45.250 --> 01:39:47.870
When we hear this a lot, especially
people who then direct themselves

01:39:47.870 --> 01:39:51.820
towards helping professions, like,
what did I like about that job?

01:39:52.100 --> 01:39:54.149
It wasn't that it had a great salary.

01:39:54.150 --> 01:39:55.370
It wasn't that the hours were good.

01:39:56.020 --> 01:40:00.620
I like that it was really helpful
to people, or there were people that

01:40:00.620 --> 01:40:04.260
were underneath of me in the hierarchy
that I could really kind of nurture.

01:40:04.270 --> 01:40:04.599
Right.

01:40:04.599 --> 01:40:09.469
And I think, right, and I love putting
food out for the birds and the squirrels.

01:40:09.469 --> 01:40:14.670
It can be a realization of self
that guides us towards consciously

01:40:14.720 --> 01:40:18.260
apprehending and thinking about
altruism as a defense mechanism.

01:40:18.800 --> 01:40:20.320
Because altruism is a defense.

01:40:20.320 --> 01:40:24.670
It's a healthy defense where if you can
do something good, you do something good.

01:40:24.670 --> 01:40:25.610
Make something good.

01:40:27.200 --> 01:40:28.829
That's the endpoint of it.

01:40:28.830 --> 01:40:31.519
Like, you don't need that to
translate into something else.

01:40:31.540 --> 01:40:32.740
It's a defense mechanism.

01:40:32.760 --> 01:40:33.460
It's a good one.

01:40:33.480 --> 01:40:35.969
And you can certainly see how it
fits with the good things we're

01:40:35.970 --> 01:40:37.330
trying to build on top of it.

01:40:37.690 --> 01:40:41.799
And sometimes through that process of
reflection, the person becomes aware

01:40:42.160 --> 01:40:46.430
of that they haven't chosen jobs by the
obvious things that even they thought

01:40:46.430 --> 01:40:48.439
they chose jobs by where is the job?

01:40:48.440 --> 01:40:49.269
What does it pay?

01:40:49.420 --> 01:40:50.540
It wasn't that.

01:40:50.540 --> 01:40:54.770
That what they really valued and what
they then started choosing upon might

01:40:54.770 --> 01:40:58.009
have been something that they weren't
aware of until they think about it.

01:40:58.009 --> 01:41:01.069
And that leads them to the
defense mechanism the same way.

01:41:01.070 --> 01:41:03.459
Another example could be
rationalization, right?

01:41:03.690 --> 01:41:08.410
Someone who thinks about their
life and they think, you know, at

01:41:08.410 --> 01:41:12.590
least kind of tell myself something
is better than it is, right?

01:41:12.590 --> 01:41:16.839
And then ultimately I'm
disappointing myself.

01:41:17.300 --> 01:41:20.870
I tell myself, like, you're
doing really well at work and I'm

01:41:20.870 --> 01:41:22.060
not really working hard enough.

01:41:22.060 --> 01:41:24.879
And then when I have that
review, I feel lousy.

01:41:25.430 --> 01:41:30.190
And that last person who broke
up with me and said, you just

01:41:30.190 --> 01:41:34.670
weren't being a reliable partner,
or that person was right, and that

01:41:34.670 --> 01:41:36.340
can lead to, oh, what's going on?

01:41:36.450 --> 01:41:39.960
I always think things are going
pretty well when they're not.

01:41:40.199 --> 01:41:43.100
That's guiding us towards
rationalization as a defense mechanism.

01:41:43.370 --> 01:41:46.590
And again, a person doesn't have
to say, ah, I conclude I am using

01:41:46.590 --> 01:41:48.530
rationalization as a defense mechanism.

01:41:48.860 --> 01:41:52.860
But there can be words put to that
of seeing a pattern in the self.

01:41:53.250 --> 01:41:56.830
When this is done as part of therapeutic
inquiry, we're often looking to identify

01:41:56.830 --> 01:42:00.740
the defense mechanisms, and that can be
great too, but it's not always needed.

01:42:01.220 --> 01:42:03.290
Defense mechanisms result in patterns.

01:42:03.900 --> 01:42:08.240
So if a person just sees the pattern,
that can be enough to recognize the

01:42:08.270 --> 01:42:12.000
pattern and either, say, follow the
pattern of altruism as a defense

01:42:12.000 --> 01:42:17.279
mechanism, or how do we work against,
how do I work against the pattern of

01:42:17.279 --> 01:42:19.240
rationalization as a defense mechanism?

01:42:20.090 --> 01:42:23.840
Andrew Huberman: Can we conclude
that patterns that we don't like

01:42:24.550 --> 01:42:28.570
are the reflection of unhealthy
defense mechanisms, and that patterns

01:42:28.570 --> 01:42:32.330
that we like are the consequence
of healthy defense mechanisms?

01:42:32.429 --> 01:42:33.880
Paul Conti: Usually, yes.

01:42:34.160 --> 01:42:36.760
It's worth some thought and some
reflection and putting together like,

01:42:36.760 --> 01:42:38.599
what exactly are the pieces of that?

01:42:38.600 --> 01:42:40.520
But basically the answer to that is yes.

01:42:41.160 --> 01:42:44.020
Andrew Huberman: In an earlier
episode, you mentioned one defense

01:42:44.040 --> 01:42:48.450
mechanism in action that is often
observed in people is acting out.

01:42:49.290 --> 01:42:52.980
This immediately sounds like
an unhealthy defense mechanism.

01:42:54.279 --> 01:43:00.300
So to keep with this concept of the
patterns are often more observable than

01:43:00.300 --> 01:43:02.480
are the underlying defense mechanisms.

01:43:02.880 --> 01:43:07.559
Would it be the case, for instance,
that if somebody has a repeated set

01:43:07.559 --> 01:43:14.390
of failures, that's a pattern, or is
repeatedly in friction in a particular

01:43:14.390 --> 01:43:17.340
relationship in their life, maybe
even just with one person, like

01:43:17.340 --> 01:43:19.930
all other relationships are going
great, but then they're in a lot of

01:43:19.930 --> 01:43:21.309
friction with this one other person.

01:43:21.400 --> 01:43:25.719
So there's a pattern from that
pattern they could explore.

01:43:27.509 --> 01:43:34.340
What is it important that they get to
a verbal identification of the defense

01:43:34.360 --> 01:43:42.329
mechanism or what sorts of steps would
one take going from a recognition

01:43:42.330 --> 01:43:47.050
of the pattern to understanding
of the defense mechanism, perhaps

01:43:47.209 --> 01:43:48.449
in a way that moves them forward?

01:43:49.140 --> 01:43:52.060
Paul Conti: The understanding of the
defence mechanism can be very helpful,

01:43:52.470 --> 01:43:53.990
but isn't always needed, right.

01:43:53.990 --> 01:43:58.380
If you can recognize a maladaptive
pattern like, oh, this is happening

01:43:58.380 --> 01:44:04.230
a lot and it's not good for me, you
become able to change that pattern.

01:44:04.969 --> 01:44:07.310
So understanding the
defense can be helpful.

01:44:07.310 --> 01:44:09.110
I mean, again, the more
understanding the better.

01:44:09.160 --> 01:44:12.030
But it's not always necessary here.

01:44:12.110 --> 01:44:15.430
I think to understand the
defense mechanism, we should

01:44:15.430 --> 01:44:16.839
first define acting out.

01:44:17.210 --> 01:44:20.250
Because we think of acting
out just hearing the words as

01:44:20.250 --> 01:44:21.929
something that's volitionally done.

01:44:22.260 --> 01:44:24.189
Right, but that's not
what we're talking about.

01:44:24.440 --> 01:44:30.110
Defense mechanisms are unconscious, so
there's an automaticity to the response

01:44:30.300 --> 01:44:32.860
that the person can see by reflection.

01:44:32.860 --> 01:44:35.430
Because this isn't
conscious choice to act out.

01:44:35.759 --> 01:44:36.579
That's something different.

01:44:36.599 --> 01:44:37.530
That's bad behavior.

01:44:38.530 --> 01:44:42.940
But what we're talking about here is the
thing that's automatic and unconscious

01:44:43.480 --> 01:44:46.450
until we bring it into our conscious mind.

01:44:46.450 --> 01:44:49.740
And acting out isn't
always dramatic either.

01:44:50.450 --> 01:44:52.010
Here's one example.

01:44:52.750 --> 01:44:58.549
So let's say in a relationship
situation, you have one person who

01:44:58.840 --> 01:45:01.259
always does the dishes, other person
does something different, right?

01:45:01.260 --> 01:45:02.389
That person does the dishes.

01:45:03.129 --> 01:45:04.629
And it's onerous.

01:45:04.670 --> 01:45:05.830
People have busy lives.

01:45:05.830 --> 01:45:06.330
It's onerous.

01:45:06.330 --> 01:45:07.350
Have to do a lot of dishes.

01:45:07.670 --> 01:45:11.340
And every time things aren't
going so well, there's a little

01:45:11.340 --> 01:45:13.190
bit of conflict between them.

01:45:13.630 --> 01:45:16.340
The other person makes
twice as many dirty dishes.

01:45:18.320 --> 01:45:23.070
This is exactly the kind of thing that
happens in relationship situations where

01:45:23.070 --> 01:45:27.570
this little thing becomes a little crack
in the door that opens more, and then

01:45:27.570 --> 01:45:31.180
there's a foot in the door, and now
there's a big problem because we act out

01:45:31.210 --> 01:45:34.729
in these ways that we're not aware of.

01:45:34.730 --> 01:45:37.940
So again, the person isn't
deciding, I'm going to do that.

01:45:38.040 --> 01:45:42.630
So that person has to do more work,
but there's an automaticity to it.

01:45:42.990 --> 01:45:47.590
And upon reflection, sometimes a person
could realize, I'm doing that, right?

01:45:47.639 --> 01:45:52.719
Or it's a real example person who
realizes, I just make much more

01:45:52.719 --> 01:45:58.840
difficulties around the house, I
make a lot more difficulties for my

01:45:58.840 --> 01:46:02.540
partner and was like, whoa, this person
doesn't want to be doing that, right?

01:46:03.690 --> 01:46:04.699
They love that person.

01:46:04.700 --> 01:46:05.890
They don't want to be doing that.

01:46:06.270 --> 01:46:09.969
But by realizing that when they can
bring a process of change and just

01:46:09.980 --> 01:46:12.570
being more self aware and saying,
look, I don't want to do that, I don't

01:46:12.570 --> 01:46:13.940
want that to be a defense anymore.

01:46:14.450 --> 01:46:17.629
If I have conscious awareness
now, I can control it.

01:46:17.980 --> 01:46:20.519
And maybe that person is
doing that other places.

01:46:20.520 --> 01:46:23.950
Maybe the person says, you know, it
kind of goes that way at work too.

01:46:24.509 --> 01:46:27.459
I could contribute to a project and
make something easier on a person.

01:46:27.460 --> 01:46:28.790
And I realize I don't do that.

01:46:29.930 --> 01:46:32.940
If I'm feeling in some negative
way, then we can go find, okay,

01:46:32.940 --> 01:46:35.110
what might the roots of that be?

01:46:35.510 --> 01:46:38.560
For example, did a parent
role model that behavior?

01:46:39.080 --> 01:46:42.979
Was that done to them where the
parent was really good to them?

01:46:43.009 --> 01:46:46.180
If they were behaving in the right way
and they make their breakfast, right?

01:46:46.190 --> 01:46:50.790
And if not, well, you make it yourself,
or, oh, sorry, there's no milk.

01:46:51.010 --> 01:46:52.130
These things happen.

01:46:52.390 --> 01:46:54.820
And then the person gets in them.

01:46:55.260 --> 01:46:58.509
Some array of circumstances, feelings,
responses, all the stuff that goes

01:46:58.510 --> 01:47:02.590
on in the unconscious mind that then
throws up to the surface this kind

01:47:02.590 --> 01:47:05.129
of acting out as a defense mechanism.

01:47:05.129 --> 01:47:08.000
So I think it's important to
point out, and it's a good

01:47:08.000 --> 01:47:10.410
example because it is unconscious.

01:47:10.460 --> 01:47:14.799
And a lot of times how we're
doing it is not dramatic.

01:47:15.590 --> 01:47:19.250
Andrew Huberman: What about salience,
this cupboard under the function

01:47:19.250 --> 01:47:26.570
of self that I think we are all too
familiar with, what we pay attention

01:47:26.570 --> 01:47:29.110
to internally and externally?

01:47:30.190 --> 01:47:33.320
I have a sort of bizarre meditative
practice that I've talked

01:47:33.330 --> 01:47:34.199
about before on the podcast.

01:47:34.429 --> 01:47:37.470
I don't know why I came up with this,
but it's more of a perceptual exercise

01:47:37.470 --> 01:47:43.359
that I do from time to time, where if I
feel like I'm too in my head, I literally

01:47:43.990 --> 01:47:45.780
focus my visual attention outward.

01:47:45.780 --> 01:47:49.099
I try and place on a horizon
or some object out there.

01:47:50.360 --> 01:47:53.960
And other times, if I'm sort of in
the world too much and I want to get

01:47:53.960 --> 01:47:58.680
back into myself, I'll close my eyes
and do a moment or two or more of more

01:47:58.680 --> 01:48:01.800
traditional, what traditional means,
what people think of as meditation.

01:48:01.810 --> 01:48:05.970
The practice involves setting aside
a minute or two and deliberately

01:48:05.970 --> 01:48:09.900
stepping through closed eye meditation.

01:48:10.270 --> 01:48:13.259
It's not really meditation again, it's
just recognize I'm a self, like here,

01:48:13.259 --> 01:48:15.129
contained within the skin of my body.

01:48:15.589 --> 01:48:17.559
Then I open my eyes, I look at my hand.

01:48:17.619 --> 01:48:19.300
This all sounds very
silly as I describe it.

01:48:20.320 --> 01:48:21.690
And then I think about a bridge.

01:48:21.720 --> 01:48:26.080
Like my perception can be split between my
awareness of self internally and my hand.

01:48:26.590 --> 01:48:32.600
Then I look out some distance, 10,
12 feet or so, and do the same.

01:48:33.940 --> 01:48:36.264
I sort of bridge self awareness
with external awareness, and

01:48:36.400 --> 01:48:38.490
I step out to the horizon.

01:48:38.550 --> 01:48:41.220
And then I sometimes like
to do the exercise of...

01:48:41.840 --> 01:48:42.680
It goes with a popular meme.

01:48:43.540 --> 01:48:48.789
We're just like this pale blue dot I
think about myself right here, but then

01:48:48.790 --> 01:48:55.010
the fact that I'm on a planet that's like
spinning in space and then right back

01:48:55.010 --> 01:48:56.760
into myself, and then I go about my day.

01:48:58.520 --> 01:49:02.460
And I developed that a few years
ago more on the basis of what I

01:49:02.460 --> 01:49:05.430
know about visual perception and
interoception, our recognition of

01:49:05.430 --> 01:49:09.080
inside versus exteroception, just
fancy language for recognition

01:49:09.080 --> 01:49:10.370
and perception of what's outside.

01:49:10.930 --> 01:49:16.930
But that's my practice of orienting
myself in life, because then I feel

01:49:16.930 --> 01:49:20.390
like I have better buffers against
what happens around me and how

01:49:20.390 --> 01:49:22.110
much I'm reacting or not reacting.

01:49:22.330 --> 01:49:23.450
That's my practice.

01:49:23.870 --> 01:49:29.570
I have a feeling it touches into a few
of these bins, but it certainly doesn't

01:49:29.570 --> 01:49:36.760
get at approaching a specific problem or
thinking about where problems might exist

01:49:36.770 --> 01:49:39.019
beneath the surface that I'm not aware of.

01:49:39.870 --> 01:49:41.560
Paul Conti: Because it's only
one part of the equation.

01:49:41.599 --> 01:49:45.640
It's paying attention to salience,
what you're doing then, you

01:49:45.640 --> 01:49:49.679
are grounding yourself in order
to change salience, right?

01:49:49.979 --> 01:49:51.520
And that is a strategy.

01:49:51.530 --> 01:49:53.460
You said, oh, maybe it's
silly or this or that.

01:49:53.460 --> 01:49:56.169
No, it's an understood and known strategy.

01:49:56.330 --> 01:50:00.929
For example, variations of that are
what people can do to prevent panic

01:50:00.929 --> 01:50:03.005
attacks, to change the salience.

01:50:03.270 --> 01:50:07.119
If the salience is I'm going inside
of me and I'm feeling panicked and I

01:50:07.979 --> 01:50:11.769
just have a feeling of awfulness, you
can change that salience by grounding

01:50:11.770 --> 01:50:13.230
yourself to the world around you.

01:50:13.700 --> 01:50:17.059
We tell people, place your hands
on the table, look at the specifics

01:50:17.059 --> 01:50:18.380
of exactly what time it is.

01:50:18.380 --> 01:50:19.729
Look at the shape of a doorknob.

01:50:20.320 --> 01:50:23.559
Ground yourself so that
you can change salience.

01:50:23.969 --> 01:50:28.820
Because now, as we move up the
hierarchy of function of self, we're

01:50:28.820 --> 01:50:31.440
getting to using the conscious mind.

01:50:32.160 --> 01:50:36.490
Things that are salient to us can be
external, they can be internal, and if

01:50:36.490 --> 01:50:39.950
they're internal, they can be conscious,
and sometimes they're unconscious.

01:50:40.170 --> 01:50:43.790
So it's not all about the conscious mind,
but we're bringing the conscious mind

01:50:43.790 --> 01:50:48.730
to bear here to think about salience,
which combined with everything else, can

01:50:48.730 --> 01:50:50.610
help us see what's under the surface.

01:50:50.929 --> 01:50:55.350
Most of the time, what we're doing
is that act of self observation.

01:50:55.610 --> 01:50:57.410
What is going on inside of me?

01:50:57.410 --> 01:50:58.699
Which can be, what am I thinking?

01:50:58.699 --> 01:51:01.710
This is how the person can
realize over and over, oh my

01:51:01.710 --> 01:51:04.360
goodness, I'm saying to myself, X.

01:51:04.360 --> 01:51:07.710
And for the first time they say out
loud and realize the thing they've

01:51:07.710 --> 01:51:10.080
said to themselves 10,000 times.

01:51:10.630 --> 01:51:12.210
Or it can be a feeling state.

01:51:13.290 --> 01:51:17.920
Wow, what's salient to me is a feeling
state, say, of vulnerability, and

01:51:17.920 --> 01:51:19.940
then everything seems threatening.

01:51:20.330 --> 01:51:26.269
So salience, it's a form of self awareness
that we could say is using the conscious

01:51:26.270 --> 01:51:31.590
mind now to tend to that garden of
self, to look at that garden of self

01:51:31.590 --> 01:51:33.599
and say, what's really growing from it.

01:51:33.969 --> 01:51:35.429
Is it all things I like?

01:51:35.429 --> 01:51:38.090
It's going to be never all things
we like because it's a process.

01:51:38.090 --> 01:51:39.580
But am I happy with it?

01:51:39.580 --> 01:51:40.980
Am I not happy with it?

01:51:40.990 --> 01:51:43.930
Are there weeds that are
coming up all over the place?

01:51:44.349 --> 01:51:45.759
That could be the intrusive thoughts.

01:51:46.250 --> 01:51:49.569
So we're using metaphors,
but it's actually very, very

01:51:49.570 --> 01:51:55.100
concrete . The salience part is
what is going on inside of me?

01:51:55.130 --> 01:51:58.719
And that's a very interesting
inquiry and informative.

01:51:58.720 --> 01:52:00.610
Right.
It's interesting because it's informative.

01:52:01.250 --> 01:52:05.130
Andrew Huberman: Do you think that's
an inquiry that's best done in

01:52:05.139 --> 01:52:12.009
meditative like states or setting
aside some deliberate time to think

01:52:12.119 --> 01:52:14.210
about what am I thinking about?

01:52:14.210 --> 01:52:15.559
What am I paying attention to?

01:52:15.590 --> 01:52:20.340
How am I allocating my thoughts
or my thoughts being allocated?

01:52:20.349 --> 01:52:23.049
I guess we have to respect the
unconscious component here.

01:52:24.349 --> 01:52:27.280
We don't just walk around saying,
I'm placed my attentional spotlight

01:52:27.280 --> 01:52:28.969
there and then my thinking here.

01:52:29.969 --> 01:52:32.280
Paul Conti: We always want to be
aware of what we, there are things we

01:52:32.280 --> 01:52:36.580
don't know that's respectful, that's
appropriate humility, because it's true.

01:52:37.140 --> 01:52:40.059
Andrew Huberman: So I'm assuming
this ratchets directly into the

01:52:40.059 --> 01:52:41.940
cupboard of behavior, right?

01:52:43.580 --> 01:52:51.380
What we're actually doing is that cupboard
best explored by listing off, perhaps

01:52:51.380 --> 01:52:56.570
on paper, in our minds, what we're doing
each day is that one way to explore,

01:52:56.570 --> 01:52:59.809
like, how am I spending my time again?

01:52:59.809 --> 01:53:04.980
Not as an efficiency exercise, but
as a way to start to explore the self

01:53:05.280 --> 01:53:11.170
and the mind for sake of building
up to more agency and gratitude.

01:53:12.900 --> 01:53:17.519
Paul Conti: The routes to most
effective self inquiry, to bringing

01:53:17.520 --> 01:53:21.290
the conscious mind to bear,
really differ widely by person.

01:53:21.290 --> 01:53:24.220
There are some people who, they
are so well served by doing

01:53:24.220 --> 01:53:25.410
that when they're meditating.

01:53:25.980 --> 01:53:29.740
There are other people who they can really
get at that when they're playing a sport.

01:53:30.250 --> 01:53:34.410
It's going on inside of them along with
the other things that they're doing.

01:53:34.410 --> 01:53:36.930
Some people find it in the shower,
or they find it when they wake up

01:53:36.930 --> 01:53:40.589
in the morning, or they find it when
they're with an animal they love.

01:53:40.830 --> 01:53:43.600
Or they might find it when they're
reading a certain kind of material.

01:53:43.600 --> 01:53:46.820
And then they read it, and then
the reading trails off and they're

01:53:46.820 --> 01:53:51.050
thinking they're a reverie, sort
of inside so how we can engender

01:53:51.059 --> 01:53:54.930
the best use of our conscious
minds is going to differ by person.

01:53:54.930 --> 01:53:57.380
But again, we can think about that,
like, what really works for me.

01:53:57.380 --> 01:53:58.590
Let me do more of that.

01:53:59.080 --> 01:53:59.440
It's interesting.

01:53:59.440 --> 01:54:03.870
We see people sometimes, I see people a
lot of the time who are, they're trying

01:54:03.870 --> 01:54:05.940
to meditate to understand themselves.

01:54:05.940 --> 01:54:07.080
And it's like not working right.

01:54:07.080 --> 01:54:10.900
And it's like I must know how to
meditate in order to understand myself.

01:54:11.400 --> 01:54:12.630
Well, it's not necessarily true.

01:54:12.630 --> 01:54:16.150
It might be I must go on more hikes
in order to better understand myself

01:54:16.150 --> 01:54:18.069
because that's how it works for me.

01:54:18.070 --> 01:54:22.059
So that process of reflection can be
very, very helpful to us because we're

01:54:22.060 --> 01:54:26.929
using our conscious mind to try and
either look inward, what is sailing

01:54:26.929 --> 01:54:30.760
it to me, including understanding
that I don't understand everything,

01:54:30.760 --> 01:54:32.099
but I can understand a lot of it.

01:54:32.429 --> 01:54:35.650
And outward, what
behaviors am I engaging in?

01:54:35.660 --> 01:54:37.430
What are my behavior patterns?

01:54:37.430 --> 01:54:44.409
And to be reflective about that, to think
about that can be immensely helpful to us.

01:54:44.410 --> 01:54:48.240
Like, how am I spending
those hours of the day?

01:54:48.240 --> 01:54:49.360
What am I doing with my time?

01:54:49.360 --> 01:54:51.690
Am I wasting my time?

01:54:51.860 --> 01:54:55.790
Do I always get mad and say
something mean to somebody?

01:54:55.930 --> 01:54:56.590
Why?

01:54:56.600 --> 01:54:58.590
Because I had a negative
thought about something.

01:54:58.590 --> 01:54:59.850
Am I doing that?

01:55:00.490 --> 01:55:06.260
Have I kind of changed since something
unpleasant happened and now I'm not so

01:55:06.260 --> 01:55:08.929
nice to someone in the household, right.

01:55:08.940 --> 01:55:13.910
Or am I taking a lot better care
of myself since I started doing X?

01:55:13.910 --> 01:55:14.240
Whatever.

01:55:14.240 --> 01:55:16.230
X may be learning more about myself?

01:55:16.260 --> 01:55:16.760
Right.

01:55:17.230 --> 01:55:20.409
Doing more of the things I
like got left that old job.

01:55:20.410 --> 01:55:22.259
It was so hard for me to leave.

01:55:22.389 --> 01:55:24.620
I do actually get myself to the gym.

01:55:25.080 --> 01:55:30.610
So it's a reflection upon self, because
a lot of what we do, we do automatically.

01:55:31.090 --> 01:55:32.630
And that's very important.

01:55:33.990 --> 01:55:37.490
The example that often is
given is, okay, think about how

01:55:37.490 --> 01:55:38.870
you last brushed your teeth.

01:55:39.570 --> 01:55:41.110
And the answer by like a blank.

01:55:41.500 --> 01:55:43.759
Because you brush your teeth
in an automatic way, right.

01:55:43.780 --> 01:55:47.969
Most of us don't remember that
because we just skip right over it.

01:55:48.250 --> 01:55:49.360
So it makes sense.

01:55:49.360 --> 01:55:51.520
It lets us think while we're doing things.

01:55:51.520 --> 01:55:55.349
It lets a lot happen automatically
in the physical world, right.

01:55:55.370 --> 01:55:57.900
Just as it happens inside
of us automatically.

01:55:58.270 --> 01:56:02.099
But we can, there have sort of
too much of a good thing where too

01:56:02.110 --> 01:56:07.070
much is happening automatically
and we want to stop and think.

01:56:07.070 --> 01:56:09.690
And it's remarkable how sometimes
when people stop and think, they

01:56:09.690 --> 01:56:14.030
might say, real example is, I don't
want to be spending five nights a

01:56:14.030 --> 01:56:17.710
week at the bar and I'm spending
five nights a week at the bar.

01:56:17.710 --> 01:56:18.099
Why?

01:56:18.099 --> 01:56:23.620
Because I go home a certain way from
work, and there's a bar along that way.

01:56:23.620 --> 01:56:26.480
And then I think, oh, I'll just
stop in and maybe see a friend.

01:56:26.480 --> 01:56:29.450
And then I know that once I get in
there, I'm going to have a drink.

01:56:29.450 --> 01:56:33.409
And I know once I have a drink, I'm going
to get three, and I'm going to have three.

01:56:33.410 --> 01:56:38.980
And I see this pattern of behaviors
and how, like, I don't decide I'm

01:56:38.980 --> 01:56:42.339
going to go to that bar instead of
going home to see my wife or my husband

01:56:42.340 --> 01:56:46.820
or my kids or whatever it may be,
and I don't want to behave that way.

01:56:47.390 --> 01:56:52.600
Because there, it's a great example of
how you can stop that from happening.

01:56:52.860 --> 01:56:55.659
But once it starts happening,
the dominoes start falling.

01:56:55.660 --> 01:56:56.740
It's very, very hard.

01:56:57.099 --> 01:57:00.020
People don't generally realize, oh, my
goodness, I'm in the bar and I've had one

01:57:00.020 --> 01:57:01.470
drink, and now I'm going to have two more.

01:57:01.530 --> 01:57:02.720
That's not the time.

01:57:03.100 --> 01:57:07.540
But the understanding, the reflection
upon behavior patterns can lead a

01:57:07.540 --> 01:57:11.820
person to stop those behaviors, to
understand and recognize them, get

01:57:11.820 --> 01:57:15.259
their arms around them, shine the
light of day on them, and then have

01:57:15.530 --> 01:57:18.639
greater agency and greater gratitude.

01:57:18.640 --> 01:57:21.020
I'm grateful I can go home to
my family, and that's what I

01:57:21.030 --> 01:57:23.130
choose to do, and I can do that.

01:57:23.130 --> 01:57:26.090
I do not have to end up at that bar,
and I'm not going to end up at that bar.

01:57:26.320 --> 01:57:27.910
I'm going to drive a different way home.

01:57:28.200 --> 01:57:31.729
And if I can't get myself to do that,
I'm have a friend in the car with me.

01:57:31.799 --> 01:57:33.879
And if I can't do that,
I'll be in the backseat.

01:57:34.559 --> 01:57:38.120
But I'm not doing that
thing I choose not to do.

01:57:38.390 --> 01:57:44.090
And it's a more dramatic example and not
an uncommon one, but we can apply that the

01:57:44.090 --> 01:57:49.759
whole way up the list, from nuances of our
behaviors down to more dramatic behaviors.

01:57:50.509 --> 01:57:55.679
Andrew Huberman: I've heard you describe
the unconscious mind and some of its

01:57:55.970 --> 01:58:02.680
other interconnected workings with the
analogy of a phantom in the driver's seat.

01:58:02.790 --> 01:58:07.879
And we're in the backseat, of
course, all within one person, right.

01:58:07.880 --> 01:58:11.679
This idea that we're just being taken
places that we don't want to go or

01:58:11.680 --> 01:58:15.150
that we know we shouldn't go or that we
can't really figure out why we're going

01:58:15.150 --> 01:58:21.450
there, and we have some idea, but we're
just not certain about what's going on.

01:58:22.450 --> 01:58:27.569
It's not necessarily related to
really destructive action either.

01:58:27.570 --> 01:58:31.640
I mean, it can be, but what you're
describing sounds to me a lot like

01:58:32.440 --> 01:58:36.910
climbing out of the back seat and maybe
sitting in the passenger seat and looking

01:58:36.910 --> 01:58:41.629
into the driver's seat and going, oh,
there's something else going on here.

01:58:41.809 --> 01:58:43.469
Of course, all of this is one mind.

01:58:45.020 --> 01:58:49.820
And in doing that, taking
some control of the vehicle.

01:58:50.030 --> 01:58:53.760
Paul Conti: It's about
understanding what is that phantom?

01:58:54.230 --> 01:58:55.310
Where did it come from?

01:58:55.720 --> 01:58:56.750
That's how we get rid of it.

01:58:57.010 --> 01:58:58.379
How do we get back in the driver's seat?

01:58:58.380 --> 01:59:01.600
We don't grab the phantom and throw it
out the door or throw it in the backseat.

01:59:02.299 --> 01:59:02.960
It's ephemeral.

01:59:02.960 --> 01:59:03.820
We can't grab it.

01:59:04.500 --> 01:59:05.689
So how does it go away?

01:59:06.039 --> 01:59:08.540
It goes away through understanding.

01:59:08.840 --> 01:59:14.300
So very common example that the phantom
in the driver's seat is trauma that we

01:59:14.300 --> 01:59:16.370
have pushed in an unconscious place.

01:59:16.400 --> 01:59:21.440
And now that whole under the
surface structure of the iceberg is

01:59:21.770 --> 01:59:25.830
fragmented, and it's sort of roiling,
and there's a big problem there.

01:59:25.830 --> 01:59:29.410
And if we go at that problem
and whatever, it's spinning

01:59:29.410 --> 01:59:30.940
off, right, that's the abscess.

01:59:30.940 --> 01:59:31.900
But it's a bad one.

01:59:32.190 --> 01:59:34.120
And it's spinning off a lot of problems.

01:59:34.280 --> 01:59:37.439
And that's why the phantom is in the
driver's seat, because healthy things

01:59:37.440 --> 01:59:41.970
are not built on top of that fracturing
and roiling part of the iceberg.

01:59:42.440 --> 01:59:43.880
We see that a lot.

01:59:44.390 --> 01:59:46.400
The phantom could also
be something different.

01:59:46.580 --> 01:59:51.390
It could be one defense mechanism
that's unhealthy that we are really

01:59:51.400 --> 01:59:55.329
over relying on, and then we can
understand it through that lens.

01:59:55.330 --> 01:59:57.150
So there are just a few examples.

01:59:57.150 --> 02:00:01.270
But if we sort of wake up in the
backseat of the car, so to speak, and

02:00:01.270 --> 02:00:05.120
the phantom is driving recklessly,
then how we get the phantom out of

02:00:05.120 --> 02:00:07.000
the front seat is by understanding it.

02:00:07.000 --> 02:00:09.130
And I always imagine, poof, it goes away.

02:00:09.420 --> 02:00:11.500
Because now it's not
driving my life anymore.

02:00:12.270 --> 02:00:13.170
I'm driving my life.

02:00:13.170 --> 02:00:13.710
It's gone.

02:00:15.330 --> 02:00:17.999
Andrew Huberman: The message that I'm
hearing over and over again in my head

02:00:18.000 --> 02:00:24.450
is that no matter how well or how poorly
any of our lives happen to be going,

02:00:25.160 --> 02:00:30.880
that by looking in these cupboards under
structure of self and function of self,

02:00:31.040 --> 02:00:34.450
we can have so much more positive control.

02:00:34.490 --> 02:00:35.019
Paul Conti: Yes.

02:00:36.680 --> 02:00:40.599
That's why ultimately, what we're
talking about is optimistic.

02:00:40.960 --> 02:00:43.360
We can't help ourselves
if we don't honor truth.

02:00:43.660 --> 02:00:47.230
And the truth is that there
are complex aspects of this.

02:00:47.240 --> 02:00:49.559
So, okay, we want to go look
at that, and we want to look

02:00:49.559 --> 02:00:52.569
at how things can go wrong, and
that's all very, very important.

02:00:52.830 --> 02:00:58.180
But that's all wrapped in the best
truth, which is that we can change it.

02:00:58.450 --> 02:00:59.639
We can make it better.

02:00:59.820 --> 02:01:05.340
That's why the self, the garden of self,
is on top of the structure of self.

02:01:05.800 --> 02:01:09.309
And the top of the function
of self are our strivings.

02:01:09.679 --> 02:01:12.609
That's what comes next after behaviors.

02:01:12.609 --> 02:01:14.560
It's like, what are my behaviors doing?

02:01:14.560 --> 02:01:15.380
What am I doing?

02:01:15.380 --> 02:01:16.740
What am I striving towards?

02:01:16.930 --> 02:01:19.340
What am I doing, literally?

02:01:19.590 --> 02:01:22.010
Am I going to a job I hate?

02:01:22.389 --> 02:01:24.359
Am I doing things I don't want to do?

02:01:24.380 --> 02:01:27.259
Am I accepting treatment
that I don't want to accept?

02:01:27.440 --> 02:01:31.120
Am I treating people in
ways I don't want to accept?

02:01:31.160 --> 02:01:33.269
How can I strive for better?

02:01:33.270 --> 02:01:36.360
And striving and hopefulness
are so intertwined.

02:01:36.540 --> 02:01:40.579
So the pinnacle of the
function pillar is striving.

02:01:41.040 --> 02:01:45.390
The pinnacle of the
structure pillar is self.

02:01:45.589 --> 02:01:48.700
And we can see how the
self, the strivings.

02:01:49.190 --> 02:01:52.300
What we're doing now is
combining the pillars.

02:01:52.310 --> 02:01:53.710
It's where it comes together.

02:01:53.730 --> 02:01:55.420
And your imagery of.

02:01:55.520 --> 02:01:57.510
That's where the geyser comes from.

02:01:57.510 --> 02:02:00.080
And we want that geyser to be healthy.

02:02:00.320 --> 02:02:04.700
It's a stream of clear, clean
water that's coming out of it.

02:02:04.859 --> 02:02:06.890
That's where our empowerment is.

02:02:07.130 --> 02:02:09.269
But empowerment is a condition of being.

02:02:09.600 --> 02:02:10.540
I am empowered.

02:02:10.630 --> 02:02:12.150
Empowerment rests within me.

02:02:12.750 --> 02:02:15.540
That's where humility
comes into the picture.

02:02:15.740 --> 02:02:18.310
Humility also something within me.

02:02:18.670 --> 02:02:19.660
I have humility.

02:02:19.660 --> 02:02:25.300
They're not verbs, but empowerment
and humility then gain their

02:02:25.300 --> 02:02:28.340
expression at the top of that geyser.

02:02:28.460 --> 02:02:34.339
When agency and gratitude, those verbs,
arise from empowerment and humility.

02:02:34.770 --> 02:02:37.429
Andrew Huberman: What you've drawn
for us is an incredibly compelling

02:02:37.990 --> 02:02:43.650
picture, because the picture
or the map is really a roadmap.

02:02:44.660 --> 02:02:46.849
It's a path to ideals.

02:02:48.100 --> 02:02:53.970
And you've been talking about these
ideals of agency and gratitude across this

02:02:53.970 --> 02:02:56.920
series, and they just encompass so much.

02:02:56.920 --> 02:03:01.410
And as you mentioned before, they are
interconnected and they are verb states.

02:03:01.870 --> 02:03:06.339
And a critical component of the
geysering up from the Pillars toward

02:03:06.360 --> 02:03:11.710
agency and gratitude are these two
components of empowerment and humility.

02:03:12.259 --> 02:03:15.670
Tell us a little bit more about
empowerment and humility and how

02:03:15.670 --> 02:03:20.470
we should view empowerment and
humility in the context of self care.

02:03:21.860 --> 02:03:27.220
Paul Conti: Well, empowerment is a state
that we can create for ourselves if

02:03:27.220 --> 02:03:30.150
we're taking care of the pillars, right?

02:03:30.150 --> 02:03:31.469
So we're looking in the cupboards.

02:03:31.469 --> 02:03:34.690
We're doing the things that make
our map clearer and clearer.

02:03:34.699 --> 02:03:37.850
This idea that, oh, that seemed like
a good path, but it gets clearer,

02:03:37.850 --> 02:03:39.559
and there's a swamp there, right?

02:03:39.820 --> 02:03:42.670
Or that didn't seem like a good
path because it's circuitous.

02:03:42.980 --> 02:03:43.340
Oh, no.

02:03:43.340 --> 02:03:44.970
But there are good things along that path.

02:03:44.980 --> 02:03:49.780
So the map gets clearer as we
tend to the cupboards in the

02:03:49.780 --> 02:03:52.009
pillars, and that empowers us.

02:03:52.009 --> 02:03:53.440
We're in a state.

02:03:53.469 --> 02:03:56.780
There's a state inside of us that
is a state of potentials that

02:03:56.800 --> 02:03:58.400
are now skewed in a good way.

02:03:59.000 --> 02:04:00.390
That's what empowerment is.

02:04:00.840 --> 02:04:02.460
It's not something that happens.

02:04:02.690 --> 02:04:06.010
It's a state that we then
bring to bear on what happens.

02:04:06.570 --> 02:04:09.000
The same is true of humility.

02:04:09.480 --> 02:04:14.410
Humility does not mean not acknowledging
things that are good about you.

02:04:15.030 --> 02:04:19.590
And we often can very much
mischaracterize humility.

02:04:19.600 --> 02:04:21.000
Like, is that person being weak?

02:04:21.000 --> 02:04:22.230
Is it false humility?

02:04:22.230 --> 02:04:24.670
Or people often who are
conscientious don't want to

02:04:24.670 --> 02:04:26.009
acknowledge good things about them?

02:04:26.009 --> 02:04:27.219
Oh, no, I'm not that smart.

02:04:27.440 --> 02:04:28.470
That's not humility.

02:04:29.100 --> 02:04:32.320
Humility is consistent with truth.

02:04:33.670 --> 02:04:36.889
So if you keep saying you're not
that smart, but the world around you

02:04:36.890 --> 02:04:38.450
tells you that you're that smart.

02:04:38.460 --> 02:04:42.149
Then acknowledge that you're that smart.

02:04:42.450 --> 02:04:45.540
That's coming through the lens of truth.

02:04:45.540 --> 02:04:48.590
And we can go down to the pillars and
the cupboards and say, okay, how does a

02:04:48.590 --> 02:04:50.660
person get to acknowledging that truth?

02:04:51.380 --> 02:04:56.510
So it's only by squaring away
the things that humility isn't.

02:04:56.830 --> 02:04:58.899
It is not denigrating ourselves.

02:04:59.130 --> 02:05:01.050
And we see that in a lot of people.

02:05:01.070 --> 02:05:01.830
I'm Humble.

02:05:01.840 --> 02:05:06.040
And then that person often tells
you why they're accepting something

02:05:06.040 --> 02:05:08.330
that's not good to accept.

02:05:08.720 --> 02:05:12.950
So humility is about acknowledging
truthfully the characteristics that

02:05:12.950 --> 02:05:15.759
you have within yourself, good and bad.

02:05:15.960 --> 02:05:19.920
And here is where we can identify
things that we're not so happy with.

02:05:20.120 --> 02:05:23.179
We have to have humility within us
in order to make ourselves better.

02:05:23.799 --> 02:05:27.190
Just like I have to say, look, I'd
like to be more fit if I'm going to

02:05:27.190 --> 02:05:30.360
then get myself in a more fit state.

02:05:30.930 --> 02:05:36.460
So saying, look, I can be a little bit
snippy with people if I'm irritated, or

02:05:36.460 --> 02:05:42.670
I can be a little bit condescending, or
I can be a little selfish at times, it's

02:05:42.670 --> 02:05:44.380
hard to admit these things to ourselves.

02:05:44.600 --> 02:05:49.165
But if we have the humility to
acknowledge those things, then

02:05:49.460 --> 02:05:53.630
we also get to have that broader
humility about just being a person.

02:05:53.630 --> 02:05:57.210
Like, wow, look how complicated
this is to navigate life.

02:05:57.210 --> 02:05:58.809
I mean, these pillars are not simple.

02:05:58.809 --> 02:06:02.019
And when we go down to the
real base elements of them,

02:06:02.020 --> 02:06:03.510
it can get very complicated.

02:06:03.750 --> 02:06:08.270
So then we have a compassion
for self and for others.

02:06:08.860 --> 02:06:11.789
Sometimes I'll say to a person, I should
be doing this, I should be doing that.

02:06:11.790 --> 02:06:13.760
They think they should be
doing something perfectly.

02:06:13.760 --> 02:06:18.280
And I will say to them, it's amazing
that we're moving forward, right?

02:06:18.280 --> 02:06:22.130
I mean, let's start with, wow,
it's not easy to be human.

02:06:22.130 --> 02:06:23.990
It's not easy to navigate this world.

02:06:24.250 --> 02:06:28.370
And that kind of humility can
then allow us to feel good about

02:06:28.370 --> 02:06:29.940
what we build on top of it.

02:06:30.259 --> 02:06:32.379
It's not easy to navigate this world.

02:06:32.380 --> 02:06:34.939
And humans are pretty
vulnerable, by and large.

02:06:35.219 --> 02:06:37.309
But I'm applying myself.

02:06:38.150 --> 02:06:42.650
And I'm proud that I'm applying myself
or that I'm perseverant, but also I'd

02:06:42.650 --> 02:06:44.080
like to be a little more compassionate.

02:06:44.460 --> 02:06:48.159
It's that sort of thing that
combines with empowerment.

02:06:48.160 --> 02:06:53.139
So empowerment and humility are
these potential states that then

02:06:53.180 --> 02:06:57.080
express themselves or become
enacted however we wish to put that.

02:06:57.080 --> 02:07:02.550
But they change into the active
verbs of agency and gratitude.

02:07:02.550 --> 02:07:06.849
And agency and gratitude
are ways of being.

02:07:07.349 --> 02:07:08.100
They're verbs.

02:07:08.150 --> 02:07:09.079
They're active.

02:07:09.339 --> 02:07:11.539
So that's the point of it.

02:07:11.559 --> 02:07:15.490
From the sense of how we
are living.How we are being.

02:07:15.800 --> 02:07:20.020
That's why agency and gratitude
is in some sense its own endpoint.

02:07:21.300 --> 02:07:25.970
But because there's a circular
aspect of this, our active

02:07:26.020 --> 02:07:28.000
being is not the endpoint.

02:07:28.190 --> 02:07:33.220
If we're being in a healthy way,
then we get to experience things.

02:07:33.870 --> 02:07:36.120
Peace, contentment, delight.

02:07:36.380 --> 02:07:39.390
We experience them because we are healthy.

02:07:39.670 --> 02:07:44.300
So we get to be through the
lens of agency and gratitude.

02:07:44.349 --> 02:07:48.820
We get to experience peace,
contentment and delight.

02:07:49.120 --> 02:07:51.540
And that makes a healthier us.

02:07:51.850 --> 02:07:55.000
The drives and their expression
are in better balance.

02:07:55.230 --> 02:07:58.300
The generative drive is
fostered and strengthened.

02:07:58.630 --> 02:08:03.319
And the drives underneath of it,
the aggression assertion, proactive.

02:08:03.880 --> 02:08:05.770
We're really using that in a good way.

02:08:05.770 --> 02:08:07.680
And we're mining all of it within us.

02:08:07.680 --> 02:08:10.500
Like, I want to bring that to bear,
and I can bring more of it to bear.

02:08:11.020 --> 02:08:12.509
That's very, very good.

02:08:12.740 --> 02:08:15.460
And the pleasure drive is active in us.

02:08:15.460 --> 02:08:16.860
I'm enjoying the things I do.

02:08:16.860 --> 02:08:18.270
I feel good about the things I do.

02:08:18.270 --> 02:08:19.629
I'm making good choices.

02:08:19.990 --> 02:08:25.460
And that state of health, what it
promotes, the pillars, the cupboards, to

02:08:25.460 --> 02:08:28.570
stay clean and clear and healthy, right?

02:08:28.850 --> 02:08:30.880
But life is life.

02:08:30.930 --> 02:08:34.980
And the slings and arrows of outrageous
fortune, the slings and arrows of

02:08:34.980 --> 02:08:39.049
life will continue to come at us
and cause us to go back and look

02:08:39.049 --> 02:08:40.610
at the pillars and the cupboards.

02:08:40.610 --> 02:08:44.620
And even if they don't come at us,
those things are never perfect, right?

02:08:44.920 --> 02:08:49.430
But that's not bad, because by tending
to them, where do we bring ourselves?

02:08:49.720 --> 02:08:54.359
We bring ourselves back to the
active verbs of agency and gratitude,

02:08:54.690 --> 02:08:56.639
the active verbs of living.

02:08:56.790 --> 02:08:57.720
And here we are.

02:08:57.730 --> 02:09:02.260
In this cycle that if we pay attention
to it, we use it to understand

02:09:02.260 --> 02:09:06.820
ourselves, we use it to improve
ourselves, can bring us to better lives.

02:09:07.710 --> 02:09:13.340
Andrew Huberman: How do you think about
a person, and I confess I've been this

02:09:13.340 --> 02:09:21.690
person, perhaps still am to some extent,
who can really have a sense of agency and

02:09:21.700 --> 02:09:29.699
gratitude in certain domains, maybe even
many domains of life, and yet feels as if

02:09:29.700 --> 02:09:33.870
there are certain areas of life that are
just so much more challenging than others.

02:09:34.460 --> 02:09:38.320
This stark contrast, like this
stuff works, can do that, but

02:09:38.690 --> 02:09:40.690
this stuff is just really hard.

02:09:41.980 --> 02:09:46.139
And maybe that continues long enough
that it almost starts to feel like, or

02:09:46.480 --> 02:09:52.009
the person wonders whether or not maybe
that part of life is inaccessible to me.

02:09:52.010 --> 02:09:54.119
It's just never going
to be successful for me.

02:09:55.720 --> 02:10:02.100
How do you think about these carve outs of
functionality and lack of functionality?

02:10:02.500 --> 02:10:07.300
That's probably not the right language,
but I think this is important because

02:10:08.360 --> 02:10:12.500
it relates directly, I believe, to kind
of narratives that we tell ourselves.

02:10:12.500 --> 02:10:14.010
I mean, they are narratives, right?

02:10:14.560 --> 02:10:20.150
I think it's important to think about
these because they are intermeshed with

02:10:20.150 --> 02:10:24.230
and perhaps even the consequence of
narratives that we have, like stories

02:10:24.230 --> 02:10:26.050
about ourselves that we have internally.

02:10:27.460 --> 02:10:33.440
And again, I'll be the first to admit that
I've felt this way for much of my life.

02:10:34.410 --> 02:10:37.750
Certain things I can do,
other things far, far harder.

02:10:37.750 --> 02:10:40.409
And sometimes it felt outside
the reach of possibility.

02:10:41.769 --> 02:10:44.610
Paul Conti: I have a very
concise answer to this one.

02:10:44.639 --> 02:10:46.009
In fact, it's nine words.

02:10:46.580 --> 02:10:49.940
Don't make yourself special
in ways that hurt you.

02:10:50.830 --> 02:10:53.929
And we tend to do that as humans.

02:10:53.980 --> 02:10:57.490
Oh, I get to have A, B and
C, but I don't get to have D.

02:10:57.790 --> 02:10:59.270
I get to have professional success.

02:10:59.290 --> 02:11:02.089
And I'm in pretty good shape and
I have a lot of friends, but I

02:11:02.089 --> 02:11:03.389
don't get to have a relationship.

02:11:03.400 --> 02:11:07.019
I mean, over and over and
over, because the relationship

02:11:07.030 --> 02:11:08.730
part is so emotionally laden.

02:11:08.920 --> 02:11:11.210
It's the part that gets carved out, right?

02:11:11.280 --> 02:11:13.960
Falsely carved out the most.

02:11:14.340 --> 02:11:18.299
But it can happen in any arena
of life where we make ourselves

02:11:18.309 --> 02:11:21.910
special in a way that really is
black magic or is being cursed.

02:11:22.539 --> 02:11:29.330
You have the machinery, the ability,
the function to go about pursuing the

02:11:29.340 --> 02:11:31.650
things you want and get them right.

02:11:31.650 --> 02:11:35.669
That sounds like a pretty good
paradigm, except about something

02:11:35.670 --> 02:11:37.309
really important to you.

02:11:38.150 --> 02:11:39.119
That can't be.

02:11:40.289 --> 02:11:44.150
We're applying the same machinery
of self because we're talking about

02:11:44.890 --> 02:11:46.450
things people want in a broad scale.

02:11:46.670 --> 02:11:48.210
Like, I would like professional success.

02:11:48.210 --> 02:11:49.500
I would like personal success.

02:11:49.500 --> 02:11:53.790
I would like to do well in my family
unit and be a good family member.

02:11:53.790 --> 02:11:54.709
I would like romance.

02:11:55.059 --> 02:11:57.039
So we're talking about areas of self.

02:11:57.750 --> 02:12:02.760
And we will make ourselves special by
carving out one and then applying black

02:12:02.760 --> 02:12:08.099
magic or some cursed state that then
takes that away from us and that creates

02:12:08.110 --> 02:12:13.660
tremendous consternation that will throw
all of those cupboards in those pyramids

02:12:13.770 --> 02:12:16.759
off balance because we don't like that.

02:12:16.940 --> 02:12:19.450
That seems mysterious and ominous.

02:12:19.840 --> 02:12:22.950
What is there that you don't
know that's about being cursed

02:12:22.960 --> 02:12:24.360
so you can't have something.

02:12:24.360 --> 02:12:28.309
And then that makes anger and
frustration in us and more likely that

02:12:28.309 --> 02:12:32.310
we'll act out or we'll be frustrated
and we start enjoying things less.

02:12:33.160 --> 02:12:36.540
Sometimes a person can wall something
off like that and they can go

02:12:36.540 --> 02:12:37.820
forward with the rest of life.

02:12:37.820 --> 02:12:40.129
I mean, it affects them,
but it's not obvious.

02:12:40.130 --> 02:12:41.650
It's not on their mind all the time.

02:12:41.950 --> 02:12:45.340
Which is why the process of self
inquiry can reveal important

02:12:45.370 --> 02:12:50.220
things like, oh, I pretend that I
don't even care about professional

02:12:50.220 --> 02:12:52.078
success, and why do I do that?

02:12:52.078 --> 02:12:56.070
It's the only thing I'm not
doing very well because I

02:12:56.100 --> 02:12:57.900
think it's impossible for me.

02:12:57.900 --> 02:12:59.250
It's not something I get.

02:12:59.580 --> 02:13:01.190
Why?
Because I got other things.

02:13:01.200 --> 02:13:02.660
So I don't get that.

02:13:02.700 --> 02:13:06.139
Okay, now we're really curious about that.

02:13:06.139 --> 02:13:11.720
And sometimes it causes very, very big
problems where a person can build an

02:13:11.730 --> 02:13:16.530
external sense of self that soothes
some of their vulnerability so they

02:13:16.530 --> 02:13:21.170
can present in a certain way, but
underneath of that, they're hiding the

02:13:21.170 --> 02:13:24.010
sadness or the pain of what is missing.

02:13:24.330 --> 02:13:27.899
But that is then sort
of eating away at them.

02:13:27.940 --> 02:13:31.500
And their feelings of self on
the inside don't match what

02:13:31.500 --> 02:13:32.780
people see on the outside.

02:13:32.790 --> 02:13:33.940
We see a lot of this.

02:13:34.210 --> 02:13:38.870
And the process of self inquiry, of self
exploration, of curiosity about self

02:13:39.179 --> 02:13:43.699
can lead us to realize what we've carved
out if we don't already realize it.

02:13:44.020 --> 02:13:48.370
Or it can help us to see that
the carve out makes no sense.

02:13:48.890 --> 02:13:53.275
It's as if you said, well, there
are nine roads around my home and

02:13:53.520 --> 02:13:55.700
they're all just regular old roads.

02:13:55.880 --> 02:13:57.920
I can drive on eight of
them, but not the 9th.

02:13:59.200 --> 02:14:01.920
You said, well, it doesn't make
any sense if they're similar.

02:14:01.980 --> 02:14:04.770
It requires the same set of skills, right?

02:14:04.770 --> 02:14:08.140
If you know how to drive the car and
have the visual acuity, why wouldn't

02:14:08.140 --> 02:14:10.250
you be able to drive down the 9th?

02:14:10.590 --> 02:14:15.969
But even though that's a very mundane
example, but it's that, that we apply

02:14:16.000 --> 02:14:20.240
to very important and emotionally
charged aspects of our life.

02:14:20.940 --> 02:14:25.350
I get to have physical fitness
and friends and career success,

02:14:25.350 --> 02:14:26.690
but I don't get to have love.

02:14:26.700 --> 02:14:28.799
I mean, we hear people say this.

02:14:28.830 --> 02:14:34.359
And that's a very powerful way
of taking us away from what

02:14:34.360 --> 02:14:36.250
we want to achieve in life.

02:14:36.259 --> 02:14:40.309
It takes us away from the active
agency and gratitude and all

02:14:40.309 --> 02:14:41.540
the good that comes of that.

02:14:42.199 --> 02:14:45.770
Andrew Huberman: What you just said makes
a lot of sense, especially the point that

02:14:46.660 --> 02:14:52.639
if we have nine roads around us and we can
drive down eight of them, why not the 9th?

02:14:53.090 --> 02:14:58.030
Because it places us back into the
verb tense and the action tense

02:14:58.030 --> 02:14:59.889
of the car like we're a vehicle.

02:14:59.910 --> 02:15:01.759
The eye that we can
take through the world.

02:15:02.230 --> 02:15:03.469
Sure, conditions matter.

02:15:03.470 --> 02:15:09.769
Maybe road number nine has boulders
on it, but it can't be that roads

02:15:09.770 --> 02:15:13.439
one through eight were all just
smooth superhighways either, right?

02:15:13.440 --> 02:15:17.139
Those had challenges and we.

02:15:17.209 --> 02:15:18.499
I'm not going to use myself as example.

02:15:18.630 --> 02:15:23.970
I, for whoever is doing this sort of
exercise, had a mind that was able to

02:15:23.980 --> 02:15:29.429
work around those boulders, right, of
challenging people, of limited finances.

02:15:30.500 --> 02:15:31.780
These are all things I've experienced.

02:15:32.559 --> 02:15:35.693
And of course, people come into the world
with different levels of challenge and

02:15:35.693 --> 02:15:37.380
privilege and accessibility, et cetera.

02:15:37.380 --> 02:15:38.660
We don't want to deny all of that.

02:15:39.500 --> 02:15:45.510
But those other eight roads are rarely,
if ever, perfectly smooth roads.

02:15:45.700 --> 02:15:48.459
Paul Conti: That's why it's
completely about the self, right?

02:15:48.490 --> 02:15:54.109
And it's the realization that if
I brought myself to bear and I got

02:15:54.109 --> 02:15:59.610
down the first eight, I can bring
myself to bear and get down the 9th.

02:15:59.810 --> 02:16:03.040
And as you're pointing out, it's not
like the first eight were easy, right?

02:16:03.059 --> 02:16:05.680
Maybe one of them was
really pretty smooth, right?

02:16:05.860 --> 02:16:08.469
But there's going to be a couple
of them in there that have raised

02:16:08.469 --> 02:16:13.679
really strong difficulties, things
to surmount and to overcome.

02:16:13.980 --> 02:16:19.210
And it's from that place of understanding
that we find within ourselves the courage,

02:16:19.240 --> 02:16:21.749
the strength to go down the 9th road.

02:16:22.020 --> 02:16:24.680
Even if we see greater
barriers, even if we're okay.

02:16:24.680 --> 02:16:29.280
I'm aware now, but I'm also aware that
I avoided that 9th road for a reason.

02:16:29.889 --> 02:16:34.720
The boulders and the potholes,
they're more severe on that road.

02:16:34.720 --> 02:16:37.380
In fact, I'm kind of worried
that it's impassable.

02:16:38.280 --> 02:16:40.960
But it can't be impassable, right?

02:16:40.960 --> 02:16:44.690
If there's boulders there, I'll
go rent some excavating equipment

02:16:44.690 --> 02:16:45.849
or I'll fill in the potholes.

02:16:45.849 --> 02:16:49.889
And that's how we get ourselves to go
forward and to acknowledge and validate.

02:16:49.930 --> 02:16:51.219
Like, I'm afraid of doing that.

02:16:51.219 --> 02:16:53.950
If I weren't afraid of doing it, I
would have done it already, right?

02:16:54.180 --> 02:16:59.459
But I now realize what the truth is and
what I brought to bear in the first eight.

02:16:59.700 --> 02:17:02.000
And I'm going to bring
myself to bear for the knife.

02:17:02.000 --> 02:17:05.610
That's also when we recruit often
resources around us where we might

02:17:05.610 --> 02:17:08.700
say, let me tell a couple of good
friends about this, or a clergy member

02:17:08.700 --> 02:17:13.930
or a therapist or a trusted other and
let me explore this more in myself.

02:17:14.090 --> 02:17:19.150
And that's often how in making
ourselves better, we engage

02:17:19.150 --> 02:17:20.609
more with the people around us.

02:17:20.629 --> 02:17:24.670
And then the support from someone else
that may help a person do that, is

02:17:24.680 --> 02:17:26.820
support given back to the other person.

02:17:26.820 --> 02:17:30.300
And this is also how we
build the beyond self.

02:17:30.300 --> 02:17:34.120
Is that the path to travel
down the 9th road, so to speak.

02:17:34.120 --> 02:17:40.559
We don't have to travel alone much of
the time, but that often, almost always

02:17:40.570 --> 02:17:42.569
wasn't in the person's mind, right.

02:17:42.580 --> 02:17:47.389
They perceive it's a three
person job to go down.

02:17:47.389 --> 02:17:48.660
It's like, great, you have two friends.

02:17:50.129 --> 02:17:50.950
Andrew Huberman: Yes, certainly.

02:17:50.950 --> 02:17:56.449
Where I've been able to travel down
certain roads, the key features have been

02:17:56.679 --> 02:18:03.309
a desire to go down that road, recognition
of the landscape, but not trying to

02:18:03.309 --> 02:18:04.949
take on the whole thing all at once.

02:18:05.799 --> 02:18:10.219
And then finding really good
people and frankly, really trying

02:18:10.219 --> 02:18:15.579
to avoid people that seemed
poisonous to the journey, right.

02:18:15.580 --> 02:18:19.900
That were going to throw toxic
things into the engine of my vehicle

02:18:20.280 --> 02:18:21.759
and that's putting a lot on them.

02:18:21.770 --> 02:18:29.930
But it just felt as if going down those
other roads was too valuable an expedition

02:18:30.420 --> 02:18:36.510
to spend time on and with people that
it wasn't helpful to spend time with.

02:18:37.610 --> 02:18:41.890
And at the same time, there have
always been good people that have

02:18:41.890 --> 02:18:45.450
presented themselves with examples.

02:18:45.590 --> 02:18:47.534
I think this is where it comes to mind.

02:18:47.799 --> 02:18:50.990
It's not always the case that
you got a friend who's saying

02:18:50.990 --> 02:18:52.629
you can do this and here's why.

02:18:52.969 --> 02:18:55.730
Or a therapist that says you
can do this and here's why.

02:18:56.080 --> 02:18:59.730
But that there are examples in the world
of like, well, this person did this.

02:19:00.850 --> 02:19:04.710
I think when we have challenges in a
certain domain, that 9th road, so to

02:19:04.710 --> 02:19:12.230
speak, I know for myself that I know I'm
in a place of futility when I start to

02:19:14.180 --> 02:19:17.890
reflexively orient towards others that
have had a problem getting down that road.

02:19:18.580 --> 02:19:21.530
Like, oh, I recognize this other
person who's been good at roads

02:19:21.530 --> 02:19:22.870
one through eight but not nine.

02:19:22.879 --> 02:19:27.570
And it occurred to me during the
course of this series, really, that

02:19:29.779 --> 02:19:31.730
why not pick different examples?

02:19:31.930 --> 02:19:35.980
Paul Conti: So if you're going on a
journey and it's a really important

02:19:36.040 --> 02:19:39.620
journey and it's a difficult
journey, but it can be awesome.

02:19:40.139 --> 02:19:41.640
Bring good people.

02:19:43.709 --> 02:19:46.840
Have them on your journey,
be on their journey.

02:19:47.440 --> 02:19:51.020
And then you think, well, why would
a person not bring good people?

02:19:51.300 --> 02:19:54.410
If I were going on a journey and it's
going to be arduous but wow, we could

02:19:54.410 --> 02:19:55.970
see amazing things along the way.

02:19:56.470 --> 02:19:58.440
I can bring a couple of people with me.

02:19:59.380 --> 02:20:03.879
I don't want to choose a couple of people
who are lazy, some who don't look at the

02:20:03.880 --> 02:20:08.720
world around them, and some that won't
be helpful to somebody else's needs.

02:20:08.840 --> 02:20:12.180
Why would one choose that?

02:20:12.540 --> 02:20:15.470
It comes back to the self, right?

02:20:15.470 --> 02:20:17.330
If a person, now we're
talking about any journey.

02:20:17.469 --> 02:20:18.750
Because it's the journey of life.

02:20:19.420 --> 02:20:22.260
If a person is choosing
people, you wouldn't choose

02:20:22.260 --> 02:20:23.330
to be on the journey with you.

02:20:23.330 --> 02:20:25.270
It's because you don't think
that you're worth better.

02:20:26.630 --> 02:20:30.370
And if you think that you're worth
better, you won't choose the people.

02:20:30.370 --> 02:20:32.199
You'll say, I want other people like me.

02:20:32.199 --> 02:20:36.549
I'm going to be diligent,
perceptive, collegial, cooperative.

02:20:36.770 --> 02:20:39.719
So I'm going to surround
myself with people like that.

02:20:40.000 --> 02:20:44.510
And if we look beyond ourselves at groups
of people and at culture, the healthier

02:20:44.510 --> 02:20:49.570
we are, the more we ally with healthy
people and the more healthy we are.

02:20:49.570 --> 02:20:53.349
Because we're making ourselves healthy,
we get healthier groups of people.

02:20:53.540 --> 02:20:57.969
The journey is better for all of us,
and this is how we can make the whole

02:20:57.969 --> 02:21:01.900
culture better .Potentially, this is how
we can make life on the planet better.

02:21:01.900 --> 02:21:03.360
But it has to start somewhere.

02:21:03.610 --> 02:21:06.500
So it has to start with the I.

02:21:08.940 --> 02:21:13.660
Andrew Huberman: I love, love, love the
message that if you're heading off on

02:21:13.660 --> 02:21:19.160
a journey that's really meaningful to
go with and make sure that you interact

02:21:19.160 --> 02:21:26.050
with good people, this is actually a
place where a reference to social media

02:21:26.050 --> 02:21:32.270
and online communities is actually
worthwhile and can be very beneficial.

02:21:32.750 --> 02:21:36.280
I think it's easy for us to
kind of roll our eyes at self

02:21:36.280 --> 02:21:37.680
help and things like that.

02:21:38.130 --> 02:21:43.740
On the other hand, there are communities
online that I consider myself a part of,

02:21:43.780 --> 02:21:49.619
but for which I and many other people
have derived a lot of strength, a lot

02:21:49.620 --> 02:21:53.330
of reassurance and confidence, right?

02:21:53.529 --> 02:21:55.260
Because a lot of people are isolated.

02:21:56.120 --> 02:21:58.890
They might have access to one or two
people in their community that they

02:21:58.890 --> 02:22:01.770
really value, but those people are
perhaps also busy with other people.

02:22:01.780 --> 02:22:07.080
Or I can remember being a student
alone in my studio apartment as an

02:22:07.080 --> 02:22:12.370
undergraduate, feeling very much against
the grain of my local environment.

02:22:13.220 --> 02:22:16.940
Too much partying for me at the time,
meaning I wasn't partying and there

02:22:16.940 --> 02:22:18.100
was a lot of partying around me.

02:22:18.100 --> 02:22:20.740
And had I been a better student
in high school, I probably would

02:22:20.740 --> 02:22:24.110
have been able to healthily engage
in that, but I just wasn't able.

02:22:24.120 --> 02:22:26.900
So, feeling pre isolated,
but knowing I was on a path.

02:22:26.900 --> 02:22:33.119
So in that case, it was one professor,
one graduate student, and a hell

02:22:33.120 --> 02:22:37.250
of a lot of books and music that,
to me, just carried me through.

02:22:37.940 --> 02:22:41.259
Nowadays, I'm fortunate to
have many more direct resources

02:22:41.280 --> 02:22:43.449
in my life of amazing people.

02:22:43.450 --> 02:22:47.489
But I just want to mention that because I
think in this discussion around self care

02:22:47.940 --> 02:22:53.810
and the various practices, I think there
are sure to be people who are that kid,

02:22:53.810 --> 02:22:58.299
that woman, that man that's alone in a
room thinking like, okay, but how, right?

02:22:58.300 --> 02:23:03.589
I see the grocer once a week and I see my
neighbor, and they don't even say hello.

02:23:03.610 --> 02:23:08.990
And how to start to access some
of these better connections.

02:23:09.830 --> 02:23:13.260
Paul Conti: Navigating the online
world is navigating the world.

02:23:13.950 --> 02:23:16.440
It comes down to understanding and choice.

02:23:17.020 --> 02:23:21.040
So if we're understanding as best we
can, we're making choice as best we can,

02:23:21.040 --> 02:23:23.320
then we'll find great things online.

02:23:23.330 --> 02:23:24.990
They're great things to find online.

02:23:25.430 --> 02:23:26.510
Same is true of life.

02:23:26.900 --> 02:23:31.770
If we're searching for something that,
for example, allies us around hatred,

02:23:31.770 --> 02:23:35.740
around acting out, around things that make
us unhappy, even around commiseration,

02:23:35.929 --> 02:23:40.000
instead of thinking about how we can
make things better, then we bring

02:23:40.000 --> 02:23:41.470
ourselves in a different direction.

02:23:41.980 --> 02:23:42.720
That's life.

02:23:43.180 --> 02:23:47.130
If we understand and we choose
as best we can, we will lead

02:23:47.140 --> 02:23:49.320
ourself to better places.

02:23:50.290 --> 02:23:53.820
Andrew Huberman: Such an important
message and is a perfect segue into a

02:23:53.820 --> 02:23:59.510
question that I, and I'm certain many,
many other people have about anger.

02:24:00.039 --> 02:24:02.850
And not just anger from
interpersonal conflict.

02:24:02.890 --> 02:24:06.460
Like somebody said something and
it really upset me, but stuff that

02:24:06.460 --> 02:24:11.180
we see, stuff that we observe in
the world, it could be acts against

02:24:11.180 --> 02:24:15.119
other people, words against other
people, or that we take reference to.

02:24:15.509 --> 02:24:23.390
And I think many people feel yanked around
by, even dragged by something they see

02:24:23.660 --> 02:24:25.370
and they can't get it out of their head.

02:24:25.690 --> 02:24:29.840
Now, there could be all sorts of
reasons related to each and all of us,

02:24:29.840 --> 02:24:32.869
why we can't get it out of our head,
work that we need to do, et cetera.

02:24:33.610 --> 02:24:39.029
But according to the map of mental health
that you've laid out for us, things

02:24:39.029 --> 02:24:44.220
that get in the way of that generative
drive are really quite poisonous to

02:24:44.220 --> 02:24:47.040
our well being and the well being of
the world, because that generative

02:24:47.050 --> 02:24:53.490
drive is about learning, creation, and
tends to be pro social in so many ways.

02:24:54.350 --> 02:24:58.740
Tell us about anger and how from a frame
of reference, of trying to engage in self

02:24:58.740 --> 02:25:04.420
care, we should think about our anger
and work with our anger in ways that can

02:25:04.460 --> 02:25:06.500
perhaps even help us and not harm us.

02:25:08.190 --> 02:25:10.969
Paul Conti: In order to really
understand this, and this is so

02:25:11.049 --> 02:25:14.000
important, we have to define three words.

02:25:14.000 --> 02:25:16.769
And the word to start with is affect.

02:25:17.090 --> 02:25:23.550
So affect is aroused in us, meaning
we don't have control over it.

02:25:23.920 --> 02:25:26.230
So anger is an affect.

02:25:26.299 --> 02:25:27.610
It is aroused in us.

02:25:27.860 --> 02:25:32.230
The idea being that if a person is
walking down the street and someone

02:25:32.230 --> 02:25:35.580
jumps in front of them and shoves
them, anger is aroused in them.

02:25:35.590 --> 02:25:37.570
They don't choose to be angry.

02:25:37.580 --> 02:25:41.840
In fact, the body reacts and has all sorts
of fight or flight responses before the

02:25:41.840 --> 02:25:44.400
person even realizes that they're angry.

02:25:44.620 --> 02:25:49.570
So we can't control what is aroused
in us in the immediate term.

02:25:49.580 --> 02:25:50.230
We can.

02:25:50.240 --> 02:25:51.280
In the longer term.

02:25:51.660 --> 02:25:55.690
If I have a short fuse and I get
angry really easily, I can't really

02:25:55.690 --> 02:25:57.279
control that in the next ten minutes.

02:25:58.069 --> 02:26:00.970
Meaning the affect that's aroused in
me, I can do different things with it,

02:26:00.990 --> 02:26:03.149
but I can't change what's created in me.

02:26:03.429 --> 02:26:06.809
But if I'm living a better life,
taking better care of myself, the

02:26:07.280 --> 02:26:08.650
generative drive is better expressed.

02:26:08.750 --> 02:26:10.170
I have more pleasure in my life.

02:26:10.470 --> 02:26:13.810
Then what happens is the
mechanisms that arouse so much

02:26:13.830 --> 02:26:15.850
anger start to arouse less anger.

02:26:16.210 --> 02:26:22.289
So by taking care of ourselves, we arouse
less anger, but anger is aroused in us.

02:26:22.580 --> 02:26:26.670
Okay, the next word is feeling.

02:26:26.980 --> 02:26:30.210
Right again, there are different
definitions for these words, but the way

02:26:30.210 --> 02:26:33.190
we're defining them, affect is aroused.

02:26:33.940 --> 02:26:39.789
Feeling is when we take that affect
and we relate it to the self, it's the

02:26:39.790 --> 02:26:42.179
next thing that happens on the way up.

02:26:42.190 --> 02:26:45.720
Because the arousal of affect
is very deep in the brain as it

02:26:45.720 --> 02:26:49.810
comes up, the next thing it does
is relate that affect to self.

02:26:50.160 --> 02:26:54.759
So this is where the classic
example of a person who spills

02:26:54.759 --> 02:26:56.500
something, they are angry.

02:26:56.500 --> 02:26:59.610
That thing is spilled,
it raises anger in them.

02:26:59.610 --> 02:27:02.260
Then they become aware, and
they match the anger to self

02:27:02.260 --> 02:27:04.269
and say, what a dummy, right?

02:27:04.270 --> 02:27:04.880
What a jerk.

02:27:04.880 --> 02:27:06.030
I'll never do anything, right.

02:27:06.320 --> 02:27:07.410
They say it inside, right?

02:27:07.410 --> 02:27:11.330
Because the anger gets
enacted against the self.

02:27:11.330 --> 02:27:14.910
Now, how would we like that to
go where the person is taking

02:27:14.910 --> 02:27:15.920
better care of themselves?

02:27:15.929 --> 02:27:20.050
So when they spill something,
less anger is aroused.

02:27:20.070 --> 02:27:23.100
And by the time it gets to
consciousness, there's less anger,

02:27:23.100 --> 02:27:26.560
so it's easier to manage, and
there's a stronger sense of self.

02:27:26.560 --> 02:27:30.540
All the other aspects of the pillars
and the cupboards are in a good place.

02:27:30.799 --> 02:27:35.329
Then the person is better able to
manage what anger makes it to feeling

02:27:35.340 --> 02:27:38.757
and then to say, okay, everybody
spills something now and then, or

02:27:38.830 --> 02:27:39.840
whatever, and then to clean it up.

02:27:39.840 --> 02:27:43.250
And the person doesn't have to
enact the anger towards themselves.

02:27:44.160 --> 02:27:47.020
So affect feeling and then emotion.

02:27:47.360 --> 02:27:51.989
So emotion is when we relate
the affect and the feeling to

02:27:52.029 --> 02:27:54.210
others in the world around us.

02:27:54.490 --> 02:28:00.580
So, for example, a person might spill
something and then it arouses anger, and

02:28:00.580 --> 02:28:04.870
now they get to the feeling part, but
they have a set of unhealthy defenses,

02:28:04.870 --> 02:28:08.160
and they don't think they're responsible
for things they're responsible for.

02:28:08.429 --> 02:28:14.589
So they just keep that load of
anger, right, that affect upwards

02:28:14.649 --> 02:28:16.050
until they get to emotion.

02:28:16.060 --> 02:28:18.470
And then they decide, that
wasn't my fault, it was yours.

02:28:19.150 --> 02:28:23.399
And that's why maybe they kick the
dog or they slap somebody or they say

02:28:23.400 --> 02:28:25.840
something mean like, this happens.

02:28:26.090 --> 02:28:30.600
So if it happens a lot, like this is
part and parcel of what's going on in us.

02:28:30.660 --> 02:28:32.520
A lot about negative emotions.

02:28:32.810 --> 02:28:36.630
There can be dramatic examples, but
there's smaller examples that are

02:28:36.630 --> 02:28:39.199
winding their way through our lives.

02:28:39.670 --> 02:28:44.720
And the better we take care of ourselves,
the less aroused negative affect we

02:28:44.720 --> 02:28:47.140
have and the better we cope with it.

02:28:47.150 --> 02:28:52.380
When it gets to the level of the I and
when it gets to the level of the you.

02:28:52.900 --> 02:28:57.520
And if we think about prosocial
collaborative behaviors versus the

02:28:57.520 --> 02:29:03.370
inaction of anger on a large scale, if
by the time it gets to you there's still

02:29:03.380 --> 02:29:07.740
a lot of anger there, it is very easy
to then paint with a broad brush, right?

02:29:07.740 --> 02:29:10.490
Oh, the problems are that
demographic, the problems are

02:29:10.490 --> 02:29:12.429
those people who aren't like me.

02:29:12.650 --> 02:29:16.909
That's where anger is
at its most dangerous.

02:29:17.200 --> 02:29:21.980
So the idea of having the negative affect
under control, having the understanding

02:29:22.000 --> 02:29:28.449
and the control mechanisms, keeps us from
getting to that broader level, the level

02:29:28.470 --> 02:29:34.400
of you, and then working in ways that
are not pro social but are antisocial.

02:29:34.400 --> 02:29:37.710
And this, I think, also relates
to what we can find online.

02:29:37.950 --> 02:29:40.800
We can find online everything
we can find in the world.

02:29:41.029 --> 02:29:42.490
So then we have a choice.

02:29:42.490 --> 02:29:45.720
Are we going to work on understanding
what choices are we going to make

02:29:45.760 --> 02:29:48.160
about how we're engaging in the world?

02:29:48.180 --> 02:29:51.939
And if we're choosing the good things,
we're taking better care of ourselves

02:29:51.940 --> 02:29:55.520
and we're better sort of citizens
of our relationships, of our family

02:29:55.520 --> 02:29:57.720
units, and ultimately of our societies.

02:29:59.609 --> 02:30:02.130
Andrew Huberman: I've observed
anger directed my way.

02:30:02.200 --> 02:30:05.040
Certainly I'm far, far from perfect.

02:30:05.050 --> 02:30:07.970
I have thousands of flaws, and
I've directed anger towards others

02:30:07.970 --> 02:30:09.470
in ways that I wish I hadn't.

02:30:11.130 --> 02:30:14.569
A common observance I've had about
myself and others is that when angry,

02:30:15.290 --> 02:30:17.390
a lot of valuable time is wasted.

02:30:17.799 --> 02:30:22.130
Instead of placing my efforts within
the generative drive, creating

02:30:22.130 --> 02:30:25.840
things that I really value, the
anger becomes an immense distraction.

02:30:26.480 --> 02:30:31.879
And I've seen this a lot, not just on
university campuses, but one place I

02:30:31.889 --> 02:30:36.499
have seen it is when I was a graduate
student or postdoc, there would be

02:30:36.500 --> 02:30:41.869
some interaction either between them
in the laboratory I was in, although

02:30:42.000 --> 02:30:47.360
rarely, but more often it was about
some interaction between a student or

02:30:47.360 --> 02:30:48.969
postdoc and someone in the outside world.

02:30:48.969 --> 02:30:51.510
And so they'd come in and
they'd be really upset about it.

02:30:51.530 --> 02:30:55.500
And there's a tendency to try and support
one another, which I think is healthy.

02:30:55.860 --> 02:30:58.670
But then it was like this would just
continue and continue, and the person

02:30:58.670 --> 02:31:00.040
would be sitting in their chair.

02:31:00.040 --> 02:31:01.000
It was really upsetting.

02:31:01.000 --> 02:31:04.770
And sometimes these were really upsetting
occurrences that warranted taking

02:31:04.790 --> 02:31:06.710
some time and just really stopping.

02:31:07.370 --> 02:31:11.233
But often I felt like things just kept
spiraling up and spiraling up, and it's

02:31:11.330 --> 02:31:15.460
like halfway through the day, and again,
I'm not immune from this, but I observed

02:31:15.460 --> 02:31:17.260
it more than I felt it, certainly.

02:31:18.800 --> 02:31:21.310
It's like, wow, that's a
lot of time wasted, days, or

02:31:23.590 --> 02:31:24.800
perhaps even weeks and months.

02:31:24.800 --> 02:31:27.220
And then there's the sleep
loss that goes with anger.

02:31:28.250 --> 02:31:31.650
I think that's one of the things about
social media and online communities

02:31:31.650 --> 02:31:35.450
that's new and unique, is that it used
to be when kids went home from school

02:31:35.450 --> 02:31:39.459
or we go home from work, something
might have happened there, but you

02:31:39.460 --> 02:31:41.490
didn't have access to more incoming.

02:31:42.500 --> 02:31:46.000
People weren't calling you on the phone,
telling you things that you don't like

02:31:46.000 --> 02:31:48.670
or talking about others in ways that
you don't like, whereas all you have

02:31:48.670 --> 02:31:50.100
to do now is pick up social media.

02:31:50.100 --> 02:31:54.320
And if you're not really deliberate
in how you interact with social media

02:31:54.890 --> 02:31:58.599
and on the internet and which news
articles you read and which ones you

02:31:59.070 --> 02:32:03.179
scroll past, that can be accessible
at two in the morning when you're up

02:32:03.190 --> 02:32:06.970
about t he thing that was angering you
during the day, that is new, right?

02:32:07.540 --> 02:32:11.259
And requires elevated levels of diligence.

02:32:11.280 --> 02:32:11.780
Paul Conti: Right.

02:32:12.050 --> 02:32:17.099
High levels of anger bring volatility
and confusion, and that doesn't

02:32:17.099 --> 02:32:19.190
serve anyone or anything well.

02:32:19.520 --> 02:32:21.560
Lower levels of anger can be healthy.

02:32:22.080 --> 02:32:26.740
I'm angry at that, and I want to
try and make it right, or I'm angry

02:32:26.740 --> 02:32:29.019
at that, and I'm going to have my
say in it, or I'm going to have my

02:32:29.020 --> 02:32:31.160
vote in it or whatever it may be.

02:32:31.160 --> 02:32:32.580
Lower levels of anger are okay.

02:32:32.580 --> 02:32:34.720
They can inform us, they
can guide our behaviors.

02:32:34.960 --> 02:32:39.420
But when we get to high levels of
anger, it's volatility and confusion.

02:32:39.420 --> 02:32:41.809
The person ceases to then be effective.

02:32:41.830 --> 02:32:43.160
And here's an example.

02:32:43.160 --> 02:32:47.690
When you were telling me about how you
feel when you're doing the solo podcast.

02:32:48.010 --> 02:32:52.950
And how your agency and your gratitude
are really in action, and you're feeling

02:32:54.679 --> 02:32:57.549
the peace and you're delighted, and the
generative drive is at the fore in you.

02:33:00.590 --> 02:33:04.390
And then I had said, what if we
add a little bit of anger, even,

02:33:04.410 --> 02:33:06.910
right, to kind of make the, And
then your response was like, oh,

02:33:06.910 --> 02:33:08.550
it would all come offline, right?

02:33:08.550 --> 02:33:11.719
Because there you're doing something
that calls for, like, you to really be

02:33:11.719 --> 02:33:13.740
at your best, firing on all cylinders.

02:33:13.940 --> 02:33:16.330
So even a little bit of anger is too much.

02:33:16.709 --> 02:33:20.540
But it's a good example because
it shows, like, you're able to do

02:33:20.540 --> 02:33:22.430
this thing that is so good for you.

02:33:22.430 --> 02:33:23.550
You're living in the place.

02:33:23.890 --> 02:33:27.340
If you could have all of existence be
like you feel, then you would love it.

02:33:27.340 --> 02:33:29.180
You could bring it to all
the rest of your life.

02:33:29.180 --> 02:33:32.620
Like, that's the nirvana we're going
for, and you're actively living it,

02:33:33.630 --> 02:33:37.660
but we could throw it off and ruin it
with even a little bit of anger, right?

02:33:37.660 --> 02:33:43.830
So it's an example that models for us
how higher levels of anger cause problems

02:33:43.830 --> 02:33:48.389
in situations that are not so rarefied
as that high levels of anger, maybe

02:33:48.389 --> 02:33:51.629
somebody blurting out somebody attacking
somebody, somebody saying something they

02:33:51.630 --> 02:33:53.530
shouldn't, somebody making a bad decision.

02:33:54.059 --> 02:33:58.450
Anger isn't good for us at high
levels, and we can decrease it

02:33:58.450 --> 02:33:59.910
by making ourselves healthier.

02:34:00.290 --> 02:34:03.780
Then we make less of it and we
control it better, and we keep

02:34:03.780 --> 02:34:07.500
ourselves at the low to moderate
levels of anger, hopefully low.

02:34:07.509 --> 02:34:08.700
Again, no, anger is not good.

02:34:08.700 --> 02:34:09.800
High anger is not good.

02:34:10.070 --> 02:34:11.500
Let's try and live in the low range.

02:34:11.500 --> 02:34:14.550
Occasionally, something is very
distressing, we rise up to moderate.

02:34:14.810 --> 02:34:16.950
That's where we have
anger in a healthy place.

02:34:17.860 --> 02:34:22.940
Andrew Huberman: When we set out on
this journey to explore what is mental

02:34:22.940 --> 02:34:31.410
health, I had no expectation that you
would deliver to us this incredible map

02:34:31.740 --> 02:34:36.440
of how to explore our inner territory
and that you would spell out such

02:34:36.760 --> 02:34:42.660
crisp and clear ideals of states and
ways of being and things to access.

02:34:43.980 --> 02:34:50.200
Nor did I know anything about the
generative drive and the other

02:34:50.200 --> 02:34:51.640
drives that reside within us.

02:34:52.650 --> 02:34:57.679
In thinking about self care and in
thinking about the sorts of things

02:34:57.679 --> 02:35:01.680
that people are challenged with
often I made out a little list.

02:35:01.940 --> 02:35:05.500
Not just anger, but things like
scared, embarrassed, grieving,

02:35:05.500 --> 02:35:08.140
dejected, tired, confused, stuck.

02:35:08.590 --> 02:35:12.289
And then I wrote, infinite
number of these, right?

02:35:12.390 --> 02:35:18.130
I mean, there have to be an infinite
number of challenges that people face,

02:35:19.219 --> 02:35:26.880
an infinite number of circumstances,
and perhaps even an infinite combination

02:35:26.880 --> 02:35:30.130
of those things that people face
and circumstances that can make it

02:35:30.130 --> 02:35:33.900
all seem like a giant oppressive
cloud within us and around us.

02:35:34.889 --> 02:35:42.619
And yet, what you've provided is really a
path of clarity, because it's a path that

02:35:43.490 --> 02:35:47.010
certainly includes a lot of complexity
down in those pillars at the bottom,

02:35:47.450 --> 02:35:49.110
the structure of self, function of self.

02:35:49.740 --> 02:35:53.380
But you've directed us toward looking
into that complexity, looking into

02:35:53.380 --> 02:35:58.750
those cupboards, as a way to arrive
at answers that bring us toward more

02:35:58.750 --> 02:36:05.390
simplicity, empowerment, humility, agency,
gratitude, peace, contentment, delight.

02:36:05.640 --> 02:36:12.170
And this incredibly attractive thing,
the generative drive that is really

02:36:12.170 --> 02:36:14.149
accessible to any and all of us.

02:36:14.839 --> 02:36:16.220
Paul Conti: It's there in every one of us.

02:36:17.010 --> 02:36:19.019
Andrew Huberman: In providing
this path of clarity.

02:36:19.710 --> 02:36:23.210
And again, I want to remind people that
whether or not you feel you're doing

02:36:23.210 --> 02:36:26.980
well in life, maybe even in all domains
of life, or whether or not you're

02:36:26.980 --> 02:36:33.179
experiencing challenge in any or perhaps
even all domains of life, going into those

02:36:33.200 --> 02:36:35.569
cupboards is clearly of immense value.

02:36:35.999 --> 02:36:42.760
And you've so graciously spelled out how
we can do that regardless of resources.

02:36:43.020 --> 02:36:48.460
Really, it sounds like all it requires is
a desire to be better and feel better and

02:36:48.460 --> 02:36:52.240
do better, and a willingness to explore.

02:36:52.420 --> 02:36:53.730
Paul Conti: Curiosity, right?

02:36:53.730 --> 02:36:57.800
If I had to summarize the whole thing in
two words, I would say be curious, right?

02:36:57.860 --> 02:37:00.060
Because curious opens
the door to all of it.

02:37:00.410 --> 02:37:05.350
Curiosity about self, curiosity about
life, leads to all the good things.

02:37:07.139 --> 02:37:09.740
